window.LEARNAI_INDEX = [{"u":"agents.html","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Agentic AI","b":"What AI agents are, how they are built, how the agent loop actually works, and how people direct, verify, and manage agents at scale."},{"u":"agents.html#what-an-agent-actually-is","p":"Agentic AI","t":"What an Agent Actually Is","b":"In plain terms An AI agent is a language model placed inside a loop, given a goal, and handed a set of tools it can operate. Instead of producing one answer and stopping, it decides what to do, does it, looks at the result, and decides agai"},{"u":"agents.html#the-three-ingredients","p":"Agentic AI","t":"The Three Ingredients","b":"Every agent, from a fifty-line script to a production coding assistant, is assembled from the same three parts: A model capable of deciding. It must reliably choose among available actions, produce well-formed tool calls, and recognise when"},{"u":"agents.html#the-agent-loop","p":"Agentic AI","t":"The Agent Loop","b":"Strip away the frameworks and every agent reduces to the same cycle. The model is called repeatedly; each call sees the entire history of the task so far. The agent loop. Steps 1–4 repeat until a stop condition fires. Note that the model ha"},{"u":"agents.html#what-the-model-actually-sees","p":"Agentic AI","t":"What the Model Actually Sees","b":"It is worth being concrete, because the mechanism is far simpler than the vocabulary suggests. Tools are described to the model as structured schemas placed in its context. The model emits a specially formatted block requesting a call. The "},{"u":"agents.html#training-a-model-to-be-agentic","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Training a Model to Be Agentic","b":"In plain terms You cannot turn any language model into a good agent with clever prompting. Being a competent agent — staying on task for fifty steps, recovering from an error, knowing when to stop — is a skill that has to be trained in, and"},{"u":"agents.html#the-capabilities-that-have-to-be-trained","p":"Agentic AI","t":"The Capabilities That Have to Be Trained","b":"Capability What it looks like when present What its absence looks like Well-formed tool calls Correct schema, sensible arguments, first try Malformed JSON, invented parameters, hallucinated tool names Tool selection Picks the right tool fro"},{"u":"agents.html#anatomy-of-an-agent-system","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Anatomy of an Agent System","b":"A production agent is mostly conventional software wrapped around a model call. Here is the full parts list. The parts of a production agent. Only the top-left box is a model; everything else is software you write, operate, and are accounta"},{"u":"agents.html#tools-the-interface-to-reality","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Tools: the Interface to Reality","b":"Tool design is the highest-leverage work in agent building, and it is closer to API design for a capable but literal-minded new colleague than to traditional programming. Four families dominate: Kind How it works Strengths Costs Function ca"},{"u":"agents.html#context-engineering","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Context Engineering","b":"In plain terms An agent's context window is a desk. Everything the agent can currently use has to fit on it. The craft of agent building is largely deciding, at each step, what belongs on the desk — because a cluttered desk makes an agent s"},{"u":"agents.html#techniques-that-actually-move-the-needle","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Techniques That Actually Move the Needle","b":"Compaction. When the transcript approaches its budget, summarise the older portion — decisions made, files touched, facts established, what remains — and continue from the summary. Done well, an agent can run for hours across many compactio"},{"u":"agents.html#multi-agent-systems","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Multi-Agent Systems","b":"Once one agent works, the obvious move is several. Sometimes that is right; often it is expensive theatre. The useful question is not \"how many agents?\" but \"does this subtask deserve its own context window?\""},{"u":"agents.html#how-agents-fail","p":"Agentic AI","t":"How Agents Fail","b":"In plain terms The way agents fail is different from the way chatbots fail, and more consequential. A chatbot's mistake is a wrong sentence you can read and discard. An agent's mistake is a wrong action — a deleted file, a sent email, a mer"},{"u":"agents.html#error-compounding","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Error Compounding","b":"This is the arithmetic that governs agent reliability, and it is unforgiving: Probability a whole task succeeds = (per-step reliability)^(number of steps) Steps: 5 10 20 50 ──────────────────────────────────────────────── 95% / step 77% 60%"},{"u":"agents.html#the-characteristic-failure-modes","p":"Agentic AI","t":"The Characteristic Failure Modes","b":"Failure What it looks like Mitigation Goal drift Step 40 is solving a problem nobody asked about Restate the goal in the context periodically; keep a visible to-do file Reward hacking Test fails, so the agent deletes the test — the metric i"},{"u":"agents.html#prompt-injection-and-the-lethal-trifecta","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Prompt Injection and the Lethal Trifecta","b":"This deserves its own treatment because it is the security problem unique to agents, and because it does not have a clean solution. Recall the mechanism: tool results enter the same context as your instructions. A model reading a web page c"},{"u":"agents.html#working-with-agents-the-human-s-job","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Working with Agents: the Human's Job","b":"In plain terms Working with agents is much closer to delegating to a fast, capable, literal-minded colleague who has no idea how your organisation works than it is to using a tool. The skills that matter are the ones good managers already h"},{"u":"agents.html#write-the-brief-properly","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Write the Brief Properly","b":"The single largest determinant of agent output quality is the quality of the request. Agents fail on ambiguity far more often than on difficulty. A good brief contains: The goal, and why. \"Add caching to the report endpoint because it times"},{"u":"agents.html#calibrate-trust-to-blast-radius","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Calibrate Trust to Blast Radius","b":"The right level of autonomy is not a property of the agent; it is a property of the consequences. A sensible ladder: Level Human involvement Appropriate when 1 · Suggest Agent proposes, human performs every action Learning a new agent; high"},{"u":"agents.html#make-your-environment-agent-legible","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Make Your Environment Agent-Legible","b":"Agents work far better in environments built for them, and — pleasingly — the changes are the same ones that help new human colleagues: A fast, reliable test suite. The agent's feedback loop and your verification mechanism, in one. Flaky te"},{"u":"agents.html#managing-agents-in-production","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Managing Agents in Production","b":"An agent deployed for real users is a distributed system with a stochastic component, and it needs the operational apparatus that implies."},{"u":"agents.html#observability","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Observability","b":"When an agent misbehaves, \"the model was wrong\" is almost never the actual cause. It is a truncated context, a tool that returned an unhelpful error, a permission denial the model interpreted as a missing file. You cannot diagnose any of th"},{"u":"agents.html#evaluation","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Evaluation","b":"Agent evaluation differs from model evaluation because the output is a trajectory , not a string. Three layers, in increasing order of what they cost you: Outcome evals. Did the task succeed, judged by a program? Cheap, objective, and the b"},{"u":"agents.html#cost-and-latency-control","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Cost and Latency Control","b":"Lever Effect Prefix caching Often the single largest saving on long runs — keep the stable prefix byte-identical Model tiering Small model for routing, extraction and classification; large model only for genuine reasoning Hard budgets Cap t"},{"u":"agents.html#identity-authorisation-and-audit","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Identity, Authorisation, and Audit","b":"An agent acting on your behalf raises questions that ordinary software does not. Whose credentials does it use? If it acts at 3 a.m. under a service account, who is accountable for the action? What does the audit log record — the agent, the"},{"u":"agents.html#where-this-is-going","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Where This Is Going","b":"Forecasting in this field ages badly, so treat the following as informed extrapolation, not prediction. The direction of travel, as of 2026: Established Agents are the default product form Coding assistants that edit repositories and run te"},{"u":"agents.html#key-takeaways","p":"Agentic AI","t":"Key takeaways","b":"An agent is a model in a loop with tools, where the model — not a script — decides the next step. If you can draw the flowchart in advance, build a workflow instead; it will be cheaper and more reliable. The context window is the agent's en"},{"u":"economics.html","p":"Economics","t":"The Economics of AI","b":"How AI gets paid for: venture and sovereign capital, staying private, IPOs, circular vendor financing, open versus closed models, and how the US, Europe and China each fund and operate the industry."},{"u":"economics.html#why-ai-needs-so-much-money","p":"Economics","t":"Why AI Needs So Much Money","b":"In plain terms Most software costs very little to build a second copy of. AI breaks that rule twice over: training a frontier model costs a fortune before you have anything to sell, and then every single answer the model gives consumes real"},{"u":"economics.html#startup-financing-the-ladder","p":"Economics","t":"Startup Financing: the Ladder","b":"AI startups raise on a scale that would have been implausible in any earlier software cycle, and the reason is structural rather than exuberant: a company intending to train frontier models needs the compute budget before it can demonstrate"},{"u":"economics.html#who-actually-has-this-much-money","p":"Economics","t":"Who Actually Has This Much Money","b":"Hyperscalers (Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Meta) — funding from operating cash flow at a combined capital-expenditure scale that reached several hundred billion dollars a year by 2025–26. Their investments are often strategic as much as finan"},{"u":"economics.html#staying-private-and-staying-private-a-long-time","p":"Economics","t":"Staying Private, and Staying Private a Long Time","b":"In plain terms Companies used to go public to raise serious money. Now serious money is available privately, so the most valuable AI companies simply do not list — and that has consequences for who gets to invest, what the public gets to se"},{"u":"economics.html#how-private-companies-handle-liquidity-without-listing","p":"Economics","t":"How Private Companies Handle Liquidity Without Listing","b":"Staying private for a decade creates a problem: employees hold paper worth millions that they cannot spend, and early investors want returns. The solutions have become institutionalised: Tender offers. The company periodically organises a s"},{"u":"economics.html#ipos-and-the-public-market-exposure","p":"Economics","t":"IPOs and the Public-Market Exposure","b":"Although the model developers have stayed private, the AI trade is thoroughly represented in public markets — just one layer down the stack. Public investors have overwhelmingly expressed the AI thesis by buying suppliers : the chip designe"},{"u":"economics.html#the-notable-listings","p":"Economics","t":"The Notable Listings","b":"CoreWeave's 2025 IPO was the cycle's landmark AI-infrastructure listing: a company that converted GPU fleets into rentable capacity, financed substantially with debt secured against the hardware, with revenue heavily concentrated in a small"},{"u":"economics.html#circular-financing-the-argument-of-the-cycle","p":"Economics","t":"Circular Financing: the Argument of the Cycle","b":"In plain terms Some of the money flowing through AI goes in a circle. A chip company invests in an AI lab; the lab uses the money to buy that company's chips; the chip company books the purchase as revenue; its share price rises on the stre"},{"u":"economics.html#the-historical-rhyme","p":"Economics","t":"The Historical Rhyme","b":"This is not new, and the precedent is not encouraging. In the late 1990s telecommunications boom, equipment makers such as Lucent and Nortel lent money to competitive carriers so those carriers could buy their equipment. Revenue grew impres"},{"u":"economics.html#open-weights-versus-closed-models","p":"Economics","t":"Open Weights versus Closed Models","b":"In plain terms Some companies publish their trained models for anyone to download and run; others keep them behind an API and sell access. This is not mainly a philosophical dispute — it is a competitive strategy, and which side a company p"},{"u":"economics.html#why-anyone-gives-away-a-model-that-cost-millions-to-train","p":"Economics","t":"Why Anyone Gives Away a Model That Cost Millions to Train","b":"Publishing weights is rarely altruism. It is generally one of four strategies: Commoditise your complement. The classic play. If you sell cloud, advertising, or hardware, a free capable model that runs on your infrastructure grows your actu"},{"u":"economics.html#three-systems-the-us-europe-and-china","p":"Economics","t":"Three Systems: the US, Europe, and China","b":"In plain terms The same technology is being financed and governed in three genuinely different ways. America runs on private capital and speed. Europe runs on regulation and public programmes. China runs on state direction and cost discipli"},{"u":"economics.html#united-states-speed-scale-and-concentration","p":"Economics","t":"United States: Speed, Scale, and Concentration","b":"The American system is optimised for velocity. Capital moves fast, failure is tolerated, and the same handful of companies fund the models, the clouds, and the chips. Federal policy since 2025 has leaned explicitly toward competitiveness an"},{"u":"economics.html#europe-rules-first-and-the-capital-problem","p":"Economics","t":"Europe: Rules First, and the Capital Problem","b":"Europe has the strongest regulatory framework and the weakest scale-up capital. The AI Act is the world's most comprehensive AI law — risk-tiered, with transparency and evaluation obligations for general-purpose models and stricter requirem"},{"u":"economics.html#china-constraint-as-strategy","p":"Economics","t":"China: Constraint as Strategy","b":"Chinese AI operates under a hardware ceiling and has organised around it. Export controls restrict access to the best accelerators; domestic alternatives are improving but still trail on performance, memory bandwidth and — critically — soft"},{"u":"economics.html#unit-economics-does-any-of-this-pay","p":"Economics","t":"Unit Economics: Does Any of This Pay?","b":"Underneath the financing drama sits a more answerable question: what does it cost to serve a unit of AI, and what can be charged for it? ~10×/yr Approximate fall in price for a fixed level of capability, sustained over recent years ↑ each g"},{"u":"economics.html#key-takeaways","p":"Economics","t":"Key takeaways","b":"AI combines the capital intensity of heavy industry with the growth expectations of software, which is why its financing looks unlike anything in previous technology cycles. The most valuable model developers are private and intend to stay "},{"u":"evaluation.html","p":"Evaluation","t":"Evaluation & Benchmarks","b":"How AI systems are measured: capability benchmarks, saturation and contamination, human preference arenas, LLM-as-judge, agent evaluation, and how to build evals you can actually trust."},{"u":"evaluation.html#the-measurement-problem","p":"Evaluation","t":"The Measurement Problem","b":"In plain terms We do not have a good way to say how smart an AI system is. We have hundreds of tests, most of which are quickly aced, some of which the model has effectively already seen, and none of which straightforwardly predicts whether"},{"u":"evaluation.html#capability-benchmarks","p":"Evaluation","t":"Capability Benchmarks","b":"The public leaderboards are dominated by static datasets of questions with known answers. They are cheap, reproducible and comparable across models, which is why they persist despite their flaws. They fall into a few families. Family Exampl"},{"u":"evaluation.html#saturation-and-the-treadmill-it-creates","p":"Evaluation","t":"Saturation, and the Treadmill It Creates","b":"The characteristic life cycle of a benchmark is now well understood, and short: The benchmark life cycle Year 0 Released. Frontier models score ~30%. Human experts ~85%. Everyone agrees it is a good measure of real capability. Year 1 Fronti"},{"u":"evaluation.html#contamination","p":"Evaluation","t":"Contamination","b":"If benchmark questions appear in the training corpus, high scores measure retrieval, not reasoning. Contamination is rarely deliberate; it is nearly unavoidable at web scale. Benchmarks are posted to GitHub, discussed on forums, reproduced "},{"u":"evaluation.html#human-preference-and-the-arena","p":"Evaluation","t":"Human Preference and the Arena","b":"Since static benchmarks measure narrow things, the field also runs large-scale head-to-head comparisons: a user submits a prompt, receives two anonymous responses, and votes. Ratings are computed with an Elo-style system borrowed from chess"},{"u":"evaluation.html#llm-as-judge","p":"Evaluation","t":"LLM-as-Judge","b":"In plain terms Human grading is slow and expensive, so the industry uses a strong language model to grade the outputs of other language models. It works far better than it has any right to, and it fails in specific, well-documented ways tha"},{"u":"evaluation.html#evaluating-agents","p":"Evaluation","t":"Evaluating Agents","b":"Agent evaluation is harder than model evaluation in kind, not just in degree. The output is a trajectory — a sequence of decisions and actions in a stateful environment — and there are many acceptable paths to the same outcome and many unac"},{"u":"evaluation.html#safety-and-dangerous-capability-evaluation","p":"Evaluation","t":"Safety and Dangerous-Capability Evaluation","b":"A separate evaluation discipline asks not \"how capable is this?\" but \"what could go wrong?\" It splits into three activities that answer different questions: Behavioural safety evals. Does the model refuse clearly harmful requests, resist ma"},{"u":"evaluation.html#the-statistics-nobody-runs","p":"Evaluation","t":"The Statistics Nobody Runs","b":"In plain terms Most reported differences between models are within the noise. A benchmark of 200 questions cannot distinguish 71% from 74%, and a great deal of published comparison — and nearly all social-media comparison — ignores this com"},{"u":"evaluation.html#building-evaluations-you-can-trust","p":"Evaluation","t":"Building Evaluations You Can Trust","b":"For anyone building on top of models, the practical conclusion of this page is blunt: public benchmarks tell you which models to shortlist, and nothing more. The evaluation that determines whether your system works has to be built from your"},{"u":"evaluation.html#key-takeaways","p":"Evaluation","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Benchmarks saturate, get contaminated, and become targets. Treat any single score as weak evidence with an expiry date. A benchmark number without its protocol — shots, reasoning budget, attempts, scaffolding, grader — is not a measurement."},{"u":"foundry.html","p":"Foundries","t":"Semiconductor Foundries","b":"Semiconductor Foundries — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"foundry.html#what-a-foundry-does","p":"Foundries","t":"What a Foundry Does","b":"In plain terms A chip is a pattern of tens of billions of switches printed onto a slice of silicon, using light with a wavelength smaller than a virus, in a machine that costs more than an airliner. Only one company on Earth makes those mac"},{"u":"foundry.html#process-nodes-what-3nm-actually-means","p":"Foundries","t":"Process Nodes: What \"3nm\" Actually Means","b":"Process node names (3nm, 5nm, 7nm) are marketing designations, not physical measurements of any single transistor dimension. The gate length of a transistor in a \"3nm\" process is actually ~12–15nm. The naming convention became decoupled fro"},{"u":"foundry.html#two-structural-changes-at-2nm-and-below","p":"Foundries","t":"Two Structural Changes at 2nm and Below","b":"For the first time in a decade, the transistor itself is changing shape, and so is the way power reaches it. Gate-all-around (nanosheet) transistors. FinFET wraps the gate around three sides of a vertical silicon fin. As fins got shorter, t"},{"u":"foundry.html#photolithography-printing-transistors-with-light","p":"Foundries","t":"Photolithography: Printing Transistors with Light","b":"Transistors are patterned onto silicon using photolithography — the same principle as photography, scaled to molecular dimensions. A photosensitive material (photoresist) is coated on the wafer. A light source exposes it through a mask (a g"},{"u":"foundry.html#multiple-patterning","p":"Foundries","t":"Multiple Patterning","b":"Before EUV, foundries used 193nm ArF lasers with multiple patterning : expose a layer two, three, or four times with slightly different masks and etch steps to achieve features finer than the single-exposure resolution limit. TSMC's 7nm nod"},{"u":"foundry.html#euv-the-critical-chokepoint","p":"Foundries","t":"EUV: The Critical Chokepoint","b":"Extreme Ultraviolet (EUV) lithography uses 13.5nm wavelength light — 14× shorter than ArF — enabling single-exposure patterning of features that required quadruple patterning with ArF. TSMC began using EUV at 7nm (partially) and it is essen"},{"u":"foundry.html#how-euv-light-is-generated","p":"Foundries","t":"How EUV Light Is Generated","b":"EUV light generation sequence: 1. A tin (Sn) droplet, ~30 μm diameter, falls through the chamber at 50 kHz. 2. A pulsed pre-pulse laser heats and deforms the tin droplet into a thin disc. 3. A high-power CO₂ laser (~25 kW) strikes the disc,"},{"u":"foundry.html#high-na-euv","p":"Foundries","t":"High-NA EUV","b":"The next generation — High-NA EUV (ASML EXE:5000) — increases the numerical aperture from 0.33 to 0.55, improving resolution by 40%. Planned for Intel 14A and TSMC N2 successor nodes: High-NA EUV: NA=0.55 CD = 0.35 × 13.5 / 0.55 = 8.6 nm Ma"},{"u":"foundry.html#from-wafer-to-finished-chip","p":"Foundries","t":"From Wafer to Finished Chip","b":"The wafer-to-chip journey. Steps 3–5 (lithography, etch, doping) are repeated for each of the 15+ metal layers and multiple transistor layers, accounting for most of the 3–4 month cycle time."},{"u":"foundry.html#the-feol-beol-distinction","p":"Foundries","t":"The FEOL/BEOL Distinction","b":"FEOL (Front-End of Line): transistor fabrication - Gate oxide growth, gate stack deposition - Source/drain ion implantation (adds n-type or p-type dopants) - Anneal to activate dopants Result: transistors exist in the silicon surface BEOL ("},{"u":"foundry.html#yield-the-economics-of-imperfection","p":"Foundries","t":"Yield: The Economics of Imperfection","b":"A 300mm silicon wafer contains hundreds of die. Not every die works — random defects (particles, crystal imperfections, process variation) kill transistors. Yield is the fraction of dies that pass electrical testing: Defect density D (defec"},{"u":"foundry.html#binning","p":"Foundries","t":"Binning","b":"Not all good dies are equal. Defects that don't kill a die may disable some functional units. Foundries and chip companies bin dies by testing performance and functionality: NVIDIA H100 SXM5: Full 132 SM, 80 GB HBM — premium tier NVIDIA H80"},{"u":"foundry.html#advanced-packaging-cowos-and-hbm-integration","p":"Foundries","t":"Advanced Packaging: CoWoS and HBM Integration","b":"The H100's 80 GB HBM is not on the GPU die — it would be impossibly large. Instead, the GPU die and 6 HBM stacks are co-packaged on a large silicon interposer using TSMC's CoWoS (Chip on Wafer on Substrate) technology: CoWoS structure (H100"},{"u":"foundry.html#key-foundry-players","p":"Foundries","t":"Key Foundry Players","b":"Company Country Leading Node (2025) AI Chip Customers Market Share (revenue) TSMC Taiwan N2 (2nm, 2025) NVIDIA, AMD, Google, Apple, Qualcomm ~60% Samsung Foundry South Korea SF3 (3nm, GAA) Qualcomm, NVIDIA (some), IBM ~12% Intel Foundry USA"},{"u":"foundry.html#tsmc-s-advantages","p":"Foundries","t":"TSMC's Advantages","b":"TSMC has maintained process leadership for over a decade due to: Process R&D investment: $36B capex in 2023 alone — more than Samsung Foundry and Intel Foundry combined. Customer concentration: Serving dozens of major fabless companies buil"},{"u":"foundry.html#geopolitical-significance","p":"Foundries","t":"Geopolitical Significance","b":"The global AI compute stack has extraordinary geographic concentration: nearly all leading-edge AI chips are manufactured at a single company (TSMC) on a small island (Taiwan, 180km from mainland China). The consequences:"},{"u":"foundry.html#export-control-regime","p":"Foundries","t":"Export Control Regime","b":"US export control escalation for AI chips to China: Oct 2022: NVIDIA A100, H100 exports restricted (threshold: 600 TFLOPS FP16) NVIDIA response: A800 (reduced NVLink), H800 (reduced interconnect) Oct 2023: Thresholds tightened further (to b"},{"u":"foundry.html#tsmc-arizona","p":"Foundries","t":"TSMC Arizona","b":"What export controls actually produced Two effects, both instructive, and neither the one intended. Substitution : restricted access accelerated domestic Chinese accelerator programmes and gave them a guaranteed home market they would other"},{"u":"foundry.html#key-takeaways","p":"Foundries","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Node names are marketing. Compare density, performance per watt, defect density and packaging availability instead. EUV is the chokepoint of the entire industry: one supplier, one source of machines, and no substitute at the leading edge. T"},{"u":"glossary.html","p":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","b":"Glossary — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-action-chunking","p":"Glossary","t":"Action Chunking","b":"Predicting a short sequence of future actions in one shot — typically half a second to two seconds of motion — rather than one action per control step. Produces smoother, more decisive robot behaviour and decouples a slow policy network (5–50 Hz) from a fast control loop (200–100"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-activation-function","p":"Glossary","t":"Activation Function","b":"A nonlinear function applied to a neuron's weighted sum before passing the result to the next layer. Common examples: ReLU, GELU, SiLU, sigmoid, softmax. Without activations, a network of any depth reduces to a single linear transformation."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-adam-adaptive-moment-estimation","p":"Glossary","t":"Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation)","b":"The dominant optimiser for deep learning. Maintains a first-moment estimate (exponential moving average of gradients) and second-moment estimate (EMA of squared gradients) per parameter. Divides the update by the root of the second moment, giving adaptive per-parameter learning r"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-agent","p":"Glossary","t":"Agent","b":"A system in which a language model directs its own control flow: it chooses the next action, observes the result, and repeats, with the number of steps unknown in advance. Distinguished from a workflow, whose path is fixed by an engineer. Requires a model trained for tool use, a "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-agent-loop","p":"Glossary","t":"Agent Loop","b":"The core cycle of an agent: the model reasons over the full transcript and emits a tool call; the harness validates permissions and executes it; the result is appended to the context; the model runs again. Continues until a stop condition — goal met, budget spent, human needed, o"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-alignment","p":"Glossary","t":"Alignment","b":"The problem of making a model's behaviour match what its designers and users actually intended, as opposed to what the training objective literally rewarded. Splits into outer misalignment (the specified objective is wrong) and inner misalignment (the model learned a strategy tha"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-all-reduce","p":"Glossary","t":"All-Reduce","b":"A collective communication operation where every node sends its local data, the data is summed across all nodes, and every node receives the global sum. Used in data-parallel training to average gradients across GPUs. Implemented via ring-all-reduce or tree-all-reduce algorithms "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-attention-self-attention","p":"Glossary","t":"Attention (Self-Attention)","b":"The core mechanism of the transformer. Each token generates Query, Key, and Value vectors. Attention weights are computed as softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k), then applied to values to produce new representations. Allows every token to attend to every other token in O(n²) time and memory."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-autoregressive-generation","p":"Glossary","t":"Autoregressive Generation","b":"Generating a sequence one token at a time, each token conditioned on all previously generated tokens. LLMs are autoregressive: a 500-token response requires 500 sequential forward passes through the model. This is inherently serial and cannot be parallelised in the generation dir"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-awq-activation-aware-weight-quantisation","p":"Glossary","t":"AWQ (Activation-Aware Weight Quantisation)","b":"A post-training quantisation technique that identifies which weights are most important (those activated by large inputs) and protects them from quantisation error by scaling. Achieves near-lossless INT4 quantisation on LLMs."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-backpropagation","p":"Glossary","t":"Backpropagation","b":"The algorithm that computes gradients of the loss with respect to all model parameters. Applies the chain rule of calculus in reverse through the computation graph. Each layer's gradient is computed using the gradient of the next layer (the downstream error signal). Enables effic"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-backside-power-delivery","p":"Glossary","t":"Backside Power Delivery","b":"Moving a chip's power distribution network to the underside of the wafer, connected through the substrate, so that the metal stack above the transistors carries only signal routing. Improves density and reduces resistive voltage droop. Intel ships it as PowerVia; TSMC introduces "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-batch-normalisation-batchnorm","p":"Glossary","t":"Batch Normalisation (BatchNorm)","b":"Normalises activations within a mini-batch to zero mean and unit variance, then applies learned scale (γ) and shift (β). Dramatically stabilises training of deep networks. Replaced by Layer Normalisation in transformers because LayerNorm normalises across features rather than the"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-benchmark-contamination","p":"Glossary","t":"Benchmark Contamination","b":"The presence of benchmark questions or their answers in a model's training data, whether by direct inclusion, paraphrase, or discussion. Inflates scores by converting a reasoning test into a recall test. Countered with private held-out sets, tasks drawn after the training cutoff,"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-bf16-bfloat16","p":"Glossary","t":"BF16 (BFloat16)","b":"A 16-bit floating-point format with 8 exponent bits and 7 mantissa bits. Same exponent range as FP32 (±3.4×10³⁸), lower precision. Preferred over FP16 for AI training because overflow is rare — critical for stability with large gradients. All major AI accelerators (H100, TPU v4+,"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-bpe-byte-pair-encoding","p":"Glossary","t":"BPE (Byte-Pair Encoding)","b":"A subword tokenisation algorithm that starts with a character vocabulary and iteratively merges the most frequent adjacent pairs. After V merge operations, the vocabulary has ~V entries. Used by GPT-2, GPT-4, and many other LLMs. Produces compact representations of common words a"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-chain-of-thought-cot","p":"Glossary","t":"Chain of Thought (CoT)","b":"Intermediate reasoning text a model generates before its final answer. Substantially improves accuracy on multi-step problems. Important caveat: the visible chain does not reliably describe the computation that produced the answer — models can reach conclusions by routes their st"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-chinchilla-scaling-law","p":"Glossary","t":"Chinchilla Scaling Law","b":"Empirical law from Hoffmann et al. (DeepMind, 2022) relating compute budget C, optimal model size P, and optimal training tokens D: D ≈ 20×P at equal loss. Prior work undertrained large models relative to this optimum. The loss follows a power law: L(P,D) = E + A/Pᵅ + B/Dᵝ with s"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-circular-financing","p":"Glossary","t":"Circular Financing","b":"A capital arrangement in which a supplier invests in a customer who then uses the investment to buy the supplier's products, which the supplier books as revenue. Legal and commonplace along supply chains; contentious in AI because of its scale, and because the pattern preceded th"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-classifier-free-guidance","p":"Glossary","t":"Classifier-Free Guidance","b":"A sampling technique for conditional diffusion models. At each denoising step the model predicts noise both with and without the conditioning text, and the two are extrapolated apart. One scalar knob trades prompt adherence against output diversity; set too high it produces overs"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-clip-contrastive-learning","p":"Glossary","t":"CLIP / Contrastive Learning","b":"Training an image encoder and a text encoder jointly so that matching image-caption pairs land close together in a shared embedding space and non-matching pairs land far apart. Produces zero-shot image classification and is the mechanism by which text can steer an image generator"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-compaction","p":"Glossary","t":"Compaction","b":"Summarising the older portion of an agent's transcript when it approaches the context budget, and continuing from the summary. Lets an agent run far longer than its context window. Done badly it drops the constraint that mattered and the agent repeats completed work."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-constitutional-ai","p":"Glossary","t":"Constitutional AI","b":"An alignment method in which a model critiques and revises its own outputs against an explicit written set of principles, reducing dependence on human preference labels. Makes the governing values inspectable and editable rather than implicit in a preference dataset."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-context-engineering","p":"Glossary","t":"Context Engineering","b":"The discipline of curating what occupies a model's context window at each step of a multi-step task — what to retrieve, what to summarise, what to externalise to files, what to delegate to a sub-agent. Successor to prompt engineering as the central craft in agent building."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-context-rot","p":"Glossary","t":"Context Rot","b":"The degradation of model performance as context fills, well before the stated limit. Attention is divided among more tokens and irrelevant material competes with relevant material, so a carefully curated 15K-token context often outperforms a 200K-token one containing the same ans"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-continuous-batching","p":"Glossary","t":"Continuous Batching","b":"An inference scheduling technique that adds new requests to the active batch at each decode step as existing requests complete, rather than waiting for an entire batch to finish. Increases GPU utilisation from ~40% (static batching) to ~80%+ on typical workloads. Implemented in v"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-cowos-chip-on-wafer-on-substrate","p":"Glossary","t":"CoWoS (Chip on Wafer on Substrate)","b":"TSMC's advanced 2.5D packaging technology. The GPU die and HBM stacks are placed on a large silicon interposer manufactured on a separate wafer, connected via microbumps. The interposer provides dense metal routing (1μm pitch) and short buses to HBM stacks, enabling the 1,024-bit"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-cuda-compute-unified-device-architecture","p":"Glossary","t":"CUDA (Compute Unified Device Architecture)","b":"NVIDIA's proprietary GPU programming platform, introduced 2006. Extends C/C++ with GPU-specific constructs: kernels (GPU functions), thread hierarchy (thread/warp/block/grid), and memory spaces (global, shared, registers). The de facto standard for AI compute; AMD ROCm provides a"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-cudnn","p":"Glossary","t":"cuDNN","b":"NVIDIA's library of optimised GPU primitives for deep learning: convolution, attention, pooling, normalisation, activation functions. Provides hand-tuned CUDA kernels that exploit each GPU's specific architecture. PyTorch and TensorFlow call cuDNN internally for most heavy comput"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-data-parallelism","p":"Glossary","t":"Data Parallelism","b":"A distributed training strategy where each GPU holds a full replica of the model and processes a different mini-batch. Gradients are averaged across all replicas via all-reduce after each backward pass. Scales easily to any number of GPUs if the full model fits on one GPU; requir"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-diffusion-model","p":"Glossary","t":"Diffusion Model","b":"A generative model trained to reverse a fixed noising process. Training: add a known amount of Gaussian noise to real data and learn to predict it. Generation: start from pure noise and repeatedly subtract predicted noise. Dominant for images, video and audio; training data is fr"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-disaggregated-serving","p":"Glossary","t":"Disaggregated Serving","b":"Running the prefill and decode phases of inference on separate pools of hardware, passing the KV cache between them. Prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so mixing them on the same accelerators makes each interfere with the other."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-distillation","p":"Glossary","t":"Distillation","b":"Training a smaller student model to reproduce a larger teacher's outputs. Transfers much of the teacher's capability at a fraction of the size and serving cost, and is a principal reason capability diffuses from frontier models to open-weight models within months."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-domain-randomisation","p":"Glossary","t":"Domain Randomisation","b":"Randomising simulation parameters — friction, mass, lighting, textures, sensor noise, latency — during robot policy training, so the policy cannot overfit to any single physics and must be robust across a distribution that reality is one sample from. The main technique for crossi"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-dpo-direct-preference-optimisation","p":"Glossary","t":"DPO (Direct Preference Optimisation)","b":"An alignment technique (Rafailov et al., 2023) that directly optimises the language model on human preference pairs (preferred vs rejected responses) without training a separate reward model. Derived from the RLHF objective but bypasses the RL training loop. Simpler than PPO-base"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-dropout","p":"Glossary","t":"Dropout","b":"A regularisation technique that randomly sets neuron activations to zero with probability p during training (typically 0.1–0.5), scaling remaining activations by 1/(1−p). Prevents co-adaptation and reduces overfitting by forcing redundant representations. Disabled at inference. C"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-elicitation","p":"Glossary","t":"Elicitation","b":"In safety evaluation, actively trying to make a model exhibit a capability — through fine-tuning, scaffolding and best-effort prompting, sometimes with safety training removed. Rests on the premise that a capability present in the weights may be recoverable by a determined advers"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-embedding","p":"Glossary","t":"Embedding","b":"A learned lookup table that maps discrete tokens (integers) to dense continuous vectors. The input embedding matrix E ∈ ℝ^{V×d} has one row per vocabulary token. The same matrix (transposed) is often used for the final output projection (weight tying), reducing parameters and imp"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-euv-extreme-ultraviolet-lithography","p":"Glossary","t":"EUV (Extreme Ultraviolet Lithography)","b":"Lithography using 13.5nm wavelength light, generated by tin plasma. Enables single-exposure patterning of features requiring quadruple DUV patterning. Essential for 5nm and below process nodes. Exclusively manufactured by ASML (Netherlands). Each machine costs ~$200–380M and is s"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-fat-tree-topology","p":"Glossary","t":"Fat-Tree Topology","b":"A three-tier network topology (leaf/aggregation/core) where bandwidth increases toward the core, providing non-blocking any-to-any communication at full line rate. Used in AI training clusters to allow all-reduce collective operations between any pair of servers without congestio"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-fine-tuning","p":"Glossary","t":"Fine-Tuning","b":"Continuing the training of a pretrained model on a smaller, task-specific dataset at a lower learning rate. Adapts the model to a new domain or format without training from scratch. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) for instruction following uses 10,000–1M (instruction, response) pair"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-flash-attention","p":"Glossary","t":"Flash Attention","b":"An I/O-aware attention algorithm (Dao et al., 2022) that tiles the attention computation to stay within GPU SRAM, avoiding writing the O(n²) attention matrix to HBM. Reduces attention memory from O(n²) to O(n) and speeds up attention 2–4× on H100. Essential for training on long s"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-flop-tflops-pflops","p":"Glossary","t":"FLOP / TFLOPS / PFLOPS","b":"Floating-Point Operation. TFLOPS = 10¹² FLOP/second. PFLOPS = 10¹⁵ FLOP/second. GPU performance is measured in TFLOPS at a given precision (FP32, BF16, FP8, INT8). A multiply-accumulate (MAC) counts as 2 FLOP. H100 SXM5: 1,979 TFLOPS at BF16 (tensor core peak)."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-flow-matching","p":"Glossary","t":"Flow Matching","b":"A generative modelling formulation that learns a velocity field transporting noise to data along near-straight paths, rather than learning to reverse a stochastic noising schedule. Simpler objective, faster sampling and better-behaved training than classical diffusion; the mainst"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-fp8","p":"Glossary","t":"FP8","b":"8-bit floating-point format with two variants: E4M3 (4 exponent, 3 mantissa bits — higher precision, used for weights/activations) and E5M2 (5 exponent, 2 mantissa bits — higher range, used for gradients). Natively supported by H100/H200/B200 Tensor Cores, delivering 2× the throu"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-gate-all-around-gaa-nanosheet","p":"Glossary","t":"Gate-All-Around (GAA) / Nanosheet","b":"A transistor structure in which the gate wraps completely around stacked horizontal silicon channels, giving electrostatic control on all four sides. Succeeds FinFET, which contacted three sides. Introduced at the 2nm generation; Intel's implementation is called RibbonFET."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-gelu-gaussian-error-linear-unit","p":"Glossary","t":"GELU (Gaussian Error Linear Unit)","b":"Activation function: GELU(x) = x·Φ(x), where Φ is the Gaussian CDF. A smooth approximation: ≈ 0.5x(1 + tanh(√(2/π)(x + 0.044715x³))). Used in original BERT and GPT-2. Replaced by SiLU/SwiGLU in most modern LLMs (LLaMA, Mistral, Claude) because SwiGLU is slightly stronger empirica"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-gemm-general-matrix-multiply","p":"Glossary","t":"GEMM (General Matrix Multiply)","b":"The operation C = αAB + βC for matrices A, B, C and scalars α, β. The overwhelming majority of compute in neural network training and inference is GEMM: attention projections, FFN layers, and embedding lookups all reduce to matrix multiplication. GPU tensor cores are specifically"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-goodhart-s-law","p":"Glossary","t":"Goodhart's Law","b":"“When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.” The organising problem of both AI evaluation (benchmarks get optimised against) and AI alignment (a reward is a proxy, and a capable optimiser exploits the gap between the proxy and the intent)."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-gpu-graphics-processing-unit","p":"Glossary","t":"GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)","b":"A massively parallel processor originally designed for 3D graphics. Contains thousands of simpler cores optimised for data-parallel computation, plus tensor cores for matrix-multiply acceleration. H100: 16,896 CUDA cores, 528 Tensor Core units, 80 GB HBM3. Dominates AI training d"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-gqa-grouped-query-attention","p":"Glossary","t":"GQA (Grouped Query Attention)","b":"A variant of multi-head attention where multiple query heads share a single key-value head. With G groups, the KV cache is G× smaller than standard MHA. Used in LLaMA-3 (G=8), Mistral, and most post-2023 LLMs. Near-identical model quality to MHA with 8× KV cache reduction at G=8,"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-grpo-group-relative-policy-optimisation","p":"Glossary","t":"GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimisation)","b":"A reinforcement learning algorithm that samples a group of attempts at a problem and computes each attempt's advantage relative to the group mean, eliminating the separately trained value network that PPO requires. Roughly halves the memory and complexity of the training loop; wi"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-hbm-high-bandwidth-memory","p":"Glossary","t":"HBM (High Bandwidth Memory)","b":"A GPU memory technology that stacks multiple DRAM dies vertically and connects them with through-silicon vias (TSVs). Uses a very wide bus (1,024 bits per stack) at shorter distance than GDDR, delivering massive bandwidth. HBM3: 819 GB/s per stack; H100 has 6 stacks → 3.35 TB/s t"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-hbm4","p":"Glossary","t":"HBM4","b":"The memory generation that doubles the HBM interface from 1,024 to 2,048 bits per stack — the first width change since HBM1 — targeting roughly 2 TB/s per stack. Sidesteps the signal-integrity and power limits that constrain raising transfer rates, at the cost of substantially ha"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-hpc-high-performance-computing","p":"Glossary","t":"HPC (High Performance Computing)","b":"Scientific supercomputing for simulations, climate modelling, genomics, etc. AI training clusters derive much of their architecture (InfiniBand, parallel file systems, job schedulers) from HPC. The Linpack benchmark (floating-point dense linear algebra) is how supercomputers are "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-inference-time-compute","p":"Glossary","t":"Inference-Time Compute","b":"Improving answer quality by letting a model generate more reasoning tokens before committing to an answer. A third scaling axis alongside pretraining and post-training, and the only one paid on every query. Improves accuracy roughly log-linearly on verifiable problems and barely "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-infiniband-ib","p":"Glossary","t":"InfiniBand (IB)","b":"A high-speed, low-latency network protocol designed for HPC and AI clusters. Provides RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access), allowing one GPU to read/write another GPU's memory over the network without CPU involvement. Current generation: NDR (400 Gb/s per port, ~0.5 μs latency). Ma"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-int4-int8-quantisation","p":"Glossary","t":"INT4 / INT8 Quantisation","b":"Representing model weights in 4-bit or 8-bit integers instead of 16-bit floats. Reduces memory footprint 2–4× and memory bandwidth requirements proportionally. W4A16 (4-bit weights, 16-bit activations) allows a 70B model to fit on a single 80 GB H100. Quality degradation: INT8 ~0"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-interpretability","p":"Glossary","t":"Interpretability","b":"Research into what computations a neural network actually performs, as opposed to what behaviour it exhibits. Strategically distinctive because it could in principle verify a safety property by inspection rather than by testing. Its central obstacle is superposition; its main cur"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-itl-inter-token-latency","p":"Glossary","t":"ITL (Inter-Token Latency)","b":"Time between consecutive generated tokens during the decode phase. Typically 20–50 ms for a 70B model at moderate batch sizes. Users perceive generation as \"real-time\" at ITL <30 ms (≈33 tokens/sec). Primary bottleneck: memory bandwidth utilisation when reading model weights from"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-jailbreak","p":"Glossary","t":"Jailbreak","b":"A prompt that circumvents a model's safety training through framing, roleplay, encoding, or extended multi-turn manipulation. Distinct from prompt injection, where the malicious instruction arrives inside content the model reads rather than from the user."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-kv-cache","p":"Glossary","t":"KV Cache","b":"Stores the Key and Value tensors computed for all past tokens during autoregressive generation, avoiding recomputation at each decode step. Size: 2 × n_layers × n_heads × d_head × 2 bytes per token. For LLaMA-3 70B: ~2.62 MB/token. Long context (128K tokens) requires ~336 GB of K"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-latent-diffusion","p":"Glossary","t":"Latent Diffusion","b":"Running the diffusion process in the compressed latent space of an autoencoder rather than on raw pixels. Compression of roughly 8× per spatial dimension reduces computation by about 64× with little perceptual loss — the change that made high-resolution image generation affordabl"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-layer-normalisation","p":"Glossary","t":"Layer Normalisation","b":"Normalises activations across the feature dimension (not the batch dimension) to zero mean and unit variance, then applies learned γ and β. Formula: LN(x) = γ·(x−μ)/√(σ²+ε) + β where μ,σ are computed per sample per layer. Standard in transformers; independent of batch size, makin"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-learning-rate","p":"Glossary","t":"Learning Rate","b":"The step size η in gradient descent: θ ← θ − η·∇L. Too large → oscillation, divergence. Too small → slow convergence. Modern LLM training uses a cosine schedule: linear warm-up from 0 to η_max (~3×10⁻⁴), then cosine decay to η_min (~3×10⁻⁵) over the full training run. Finding the"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-lethal-trifecta","p":"Glossary","t":"Lethal Trifecta","b":"The combination that makes an agent genuinely dangerous: access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and the ability to communicate externally. With all three, injected instructions can cause exfiltration. The practical defence is to break one leg — commonly by removin"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-llm-as-judge","p":"Glossary","t":"LLM-as-Judge","b":"Using a capable language model to grade another model's outputs against a rubric. Fast, cheap and reproducible, with correlation to human experts often in the 0.7–0.9 range. Carries known biases — position, self-preference, length — and must be calibrated against human labels bef"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-lora-low-rank-adaptation","p":"Glossary","t":"LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)","b":"A parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique. Instead of updating full weight matrices W ∈ ℝ^{d×k}, trains low-rank delta matrices: ΔW = AB where A ∈ ℝ^{d×r}, B ∈ ℝ^{r×k}, r << min(d,k). Typically r=8–64. Reduces trainable parameters 100–1,000× vs full fine-tuning while achieving "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-loss-function","p":"Glossary","t":"Loss Function","b":"Measures prediction error. Cross-entropy loss for classification: L = −Σ y_true·log(y_pred). MSE for regression: L = (1/n)Σ(ŷ−y)². LLMs use cross-entropy on next-token prediction: L = −(1/T)Σ log P(x_t|x_{<t}). Measured in nats (natural log) or bits (log base 2). Irreducible entr"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-mcp-model-context-protocol","p":"Glossary","t":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","b":"An open standard, released in late 2024 and broadly adopted since, for connecting models to tools, data sources and prompts through a common interface. Turns an N×M integration problem into N+M: write one server per system, and every compatible agent can use it."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-mfu-model-flop-utilisation","p":"Glossary","t":"MFU (Model FLOP Utilisation)","b":"The fraction of peak GPU TFLOPS actually used for model computation: MFU = (achieved FLOP/s) / (peak FLOP/s). Values of 30–50% are typical; 60%+ is excellent. Lost utilisation comes from communication overhead (all-reduce), data loading, pipeline bubbles, and memory-bound operati"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-mixed-precision-training","p":"Glossary","t":"Mixed Precision Training","b":"Training with different precisions for different operations. Standard: BF16 activations and gradients, FP32 master weights and optimizer state. Reduces GPU memory ~2× vs pure FP32 and doubles throughput on BF16 tensor cores. FP8 mixed precision (H100+) offers further gains for fo"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-mla-multi-head-latent-attention","p":"Glossary","t":"MLA (Multi-head Latent Attention)","b":"An attention variant that compresses keys and values into a low-rank latent vector, caches that, and reconstructs per-head projections during computation. Shrinks the KV cache by roughly an order of magnitude versus standard multi-head attention while preserving more expressivene"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-moe-mixture-of-experts","p":"Glossary","t":"MoE (Mixture of Experts)","b":"A neural network architecture where each FFN layer is replaced by E independent \"expert\" networks, and a learned router sends each token to the top-k experts. Total parameters = E × FFN_size; active parameters per token = k × FFN_size. Enables very large model capacity (total par"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-moravec-s-paradox","p":"Glossary","t":"Moravec's Paradox","b":"The observation that high-level reasoning requires little computation while low-level sensorimotor skill requires enormous amounts — because perception and movement were optimised over hundreds of millions of years and abstract reasoning is recent. The organising fact of robotics"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-multi-head-attention-mha","p":"Glossary","t":"Multi-Head Attention (MHA)","b":"H parallel self-attention operations, each with its own Q, K, V projection matrices of dimension d/H. Results are concatenated and projected. Allows the model to attend to different aspects of the input simultaneously — different heads specialise in different relationship types. "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-nccl-nvidia-collective-communications-library","p":"Glossary","t":"NCCL (NVIDIA Collective Communications Library)","b":"NVIDIA's library implementing collective operations (all-reduce, broadcast, scatter, gather) over NVLink and InfiniBand. Used by PyTorch DDP and DeepSpeed for gradient synchronisation. Automatically selects the optimal algorithm (ring, tree, direct) based on topology and message "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-neural-audio-codec","p":"Glossary","t":"Neural Audio Codec","b":"A learned compressor that turns a waveform into a few hundred discrete tokens per second while retaining enough information to reconstruct natural speech. Brings audio into the same regime as text, so a transformer can generate sound by predicting tokens."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-nvlink","p":"Glossary","t":"NVLink","b":"NVIDIA's proprietary GPU-to-GPU interconnect. NVLink 4.0 (H100): 900 GB/s bidirectional per GPU. Connects up to 8 GPUs within a server via NVSwitch crossbars (DGX H100). 14× faster than PCIe Gen5 for all-reduce operations. Critical for tensor parallelism: weight shards are spread"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-open-weights","p":"Glossary","t":"Open Weights","b":"A model whose trained parameters are published, usually with inference code but without training data, data pipeline or training infrastructure. Frequently and inaccurately called “open source”: you can run and fine-tune the model but cannot reproduce or fully audit it, and some "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-over-refusal","p":"Glossary","t":"Over-refusal","b":"Declining benign requests that superficially resemble harmful ones. The mirror image of harmful compliance and a genuine failure mode in its own right, since it makes systems unusable in medicine, security research and law. Reducing one tends to increase the other."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-overfitting","p":"Glossary","t":"Overfitting","b":"When a model learns to memorise training data rather than generalise to unseen examples. Symptoms: training loss continues decreasing while validation loss increases. Mitigated by: regularisation (dropout, weight decay), data augmentation, early stopping, or more training data. L"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-pagedattention","p":"Glossary","t":"PagedAttention","b":"A KV cache memory management system (vLLM, 2023) inspired by OS virtual memory. Allocates KV cache in non-contiguous \"pages\" of ~16 tokens rather than contiguous max-length blocks. Eliminates internal fragmentation: a short request wastes no memory, and requests can share pages ("},{"u":"glossary.html#term-pass-k-and-pass-k","p":"Glossary","t":"pass@k and pass^k","b":"Agent evaluation metrics. pass@k is the probability of succeeding at least once in k attempts — the commonly reported and flattering number. pass^k is the probability of succeeding in all k attempts — the number that reflects the reliability production requires. The gap between t"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-patch-embedding","p":"Glossary","t":"Patch Embedding","b":"Turning an image into a token sequence by cutting it into fixed-size patches (typically 16×16 pixels), flattening each, projecting through a learned linear layer, and adding a positional embedding. Makes images processable by an unmodified transformer. Extends to spacetime patche"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-pipeline-parallelism","p":"Glossary","t":"Pipeline Parallelism","b":"A distributed training strategy where the model is split into sequential stages, each running on a separate GPU group. Micro-batches flow through the pipeline in a 1F1B (one forward, one backward) schedule to minimise idle time. Allows training models too large for tensor paralle"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-positional-encoding","p":"Glossary","t":"Positional Encoding","b":"Information injected into token representations so the transformer knows the order of tokens. Original transformers used sinusoidal functions. Modern LLMs use RoPE (Rotary Positional Embedding): rotating Q and K vectors by position-dependent angles so that Q·Kᵀ depends only on re"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-prefill-phase","p":"Glossary","t":"Prefill Phase","b":"The first phase of LLM inference: processing all input tokens simultaneously (in parallel, like training). Compute-bound; takes 10–500 ms depending on prompt length and model size. Produces the KV cache for the entire prompt, which is reused during decode. Time to first token (TT"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-prefix-caching","p":"Glossary","t":"Prefix Caching","b":"Reusing the computed attention state for an identical prompt prefix across requests, so repeated content is billed and computed at a small fraction of its original cost. Structural for agents, which resend a growing but mostly stable transcript at every step. Requires the prefix "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-pretraining","p":"Glossary","t":"Pretraining","b":"Training a large model from scratch on a massive unlabelled corpus using a self-supervised objective (next-token prediction for LLMs). Requires the most compute — typically 10²³–10²⁵ FLOPs for frontier models. Produces a model with broad world knowledge and language ability, whic"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-process-node-nm","p":"Glossary","t":"Process Node (nm)","b":"A marketing label for a semiconductor manufacturing generation, nominally in nanometres. Not a direct physical measurement — TSMC's \"3nm\" (N3E) has transistor gate lengths of ~12–15nm. What matters is transistor density (million transistors per mm²): N3E achieves ~172 MTr/mm². Ea"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-prompt-injection","p":"Glossary","t":"Prompt Injection","b":"Instructions embedded in content a model reads — a web page, a document, a code comment, a database row — which the model then follows. Structural rather than a bug: there is no channel separation between data and instructions in a context window. Mitigated by narrowing permissio"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-quantisation","p":"Glossary","t":"Quantisation","b":"Representing model weights and/or activations in lower-precision integer formats (INT8, INT4) instead of floating-point. Reduces memory footprint and memory bandwidth requirements proportionally. W4A16 (4-bit weights, 16-bit activations) allows a 70B model to fit on a single H100"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-rdma-remote-direct-memory-access","p":"Glossary","t":"RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access)","b":"Network technology allowing one server to read or write another server's memory without involving either CPU. Used with InfiniBand in AI clusters to achieve sub-microsecond GPU-to-GPU communication across the network. Eliminates PCIe and CPU copying overhead. RDMA over Converged "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-react","p":"Glossary","t":"ReAct","b":"The pattern of interleaving reasoning and acting — think, act, observe, repeat — formalised in 2022. Outperformed both pure chain-of-thought, which never checks reality, and pure action selection, which never plans. The structural ancestor of essentially every agent architecture "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-reality-gap","p":"Glossary","t":"Reality Gap","b":"The mismatch between a physics simulator and the real world, which a policy trained purely in simulation will exploit. Small for rigid-body dynamics such as locomotion, large for contact-rich and deformable manipulation — which is why robots learned to walk in simulation but not "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-reasoning-model","p":"Glossary","t":"Reasoning Model","b":"A model trained, typically through reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks, to generate an extended chain of reasoning before answering. Delivers large gains on mathematics, code and logic, little gain on open-ended tasks, and multiplies output tokens — and therefore cost — pe"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-relu-rectified-linear-unit","p":"Glossary","t":"ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)","b":"The most widely used activation function: ReLU(x) = max(0, x). Computationally trivial (one comparison), avoids vanishing gradients in the positive region, and empirically trains fast and well. \"Dead ReLU\" problem: neurons receiving consistently negative input permanently output "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-responsible-scaling-policy","p":"Glossary","t":"Responsible Scaling Policy","b":"A published framework defining capability thresholds tied to concrete dangerous capabilities, the safeguards required at each tier, the evaluations that determine which tier a model has reached, and a commitment to pause if safeguards are not ready. Voluntary and self-assessed; a"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-reward-hacking","p":"Glossary","t":"Reward Hacking","b":"A model satisfying the measurement rather than the goal — deleting a failing test, special-casing a grader's inputs, exploiting an environment bug. Not an occasional defect but the default behaviour of a capable optimiser given a proxy reward, and the reason environment design is"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-rlhf-reinforcement-learning-from-human-feedback","p":"Glossary","t":"RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback)","b":"An alignment pipeline: (1) Train a reward model on human preference data (ranked response pairs). (2) Use PPO (Proximal Policy Optimisation) to update the LLM to maximise reward while staying close to the SFT model (KL penalty). Produces models that follow instructions and are he"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-rlvr-rl-from-verifiable-rewards","p":"Glossary","t":"RLVR (RL from Verifiable Rewards)","b":"Reinforcement learning on tasks whose success a program can check — tests passing, proofs verifying, answers matching. Removes humans from the reward loop, so training scales with compute rather than with labelling hours. The method that produced reasoning models and practical ag"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-rope-rotary-positional-embedding","p":"Glossary","t":"RoPE (Rotary Positional Embedding)","b":"Encodes token position by rotating the Q and K vectors in attention by a position-dependent angle. After rotation: Q_m · K_nᵀ depends only on relative position (m−n), not absolute positions. Used in LLaMA, Mistral, Gemma, and most modern open LLMs. Extrapolates to longer context "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-scalable-oversight","p":"Glossary","t":"Scalable Oversight","b":"The problem of supervising systems whose capability exceeds the supervisor's in the relevant domain. Human preference feedback degrades once people cannot reliably tell which of two outputs is better. Approaches include debate between models, recursive decomposition into checkabl"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-scaling-laws","p":"Glossary","t":"Scaling Laws","b":"Empirical power-law relationships between model size P, training data D, compute C, and loss L. Kaplan et al. (2020) first quantified: L ∝ P^{−0.076}. Chinchilla (2022) refined: optimal D ≈ 20P at fixed compute. These laws allow predicting model quality and compute cost for model"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-sft-supervised-fine-tuning","p":"Glossary","t":"SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning)","b":"Fine-tuning a pretrained LLM on labelled instruction-response pairs. Typically uses 10,000–500,000 human-written or curated examples, trained for 1–3 epochs at a lower learning rate (~5×10⁻⁶). Produces a model that follows instructions and matches the style of the training exampl"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-sim-to-real-transfer","p":"Glossary","t":"Sim-to-Real Transfer","b":"Deploying a policy trained in simulation onto physical hardware. Enabled by domain randomisation, system identification, real-to-sim-to-real reconstruction and co-training on real data. Works well for locomotion and poorly for dexterous manipulation, for reasons rooted in what si"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-sm-streaming-multiprocessor","p":"Glossary","t":"SM (Streaming Multiprocessor)","b":"The basic compute unit of an NVIDIA GPU. Each SM contains 128 FP32 CUDA cores, 4 tensor core units, 256 KB register file, and 228 KB L1/shared memory. CUDA thread blocks are scheduled to SMs; each SM runs up to 64 warps concurrently. H100 SXM5 has 132 SMs. The SM is the GPU's equ"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-softmax","p":"Glossary","t":"Softmax","b":"Converts a vector of real values (logits) to a probability distribution: softmax(z)_i = e^{z_i} / Σ_j e^{z_j}. Used in attention (row-wise) and output layer (to produce next-token probabilities). Numerically stabilised by subtracting max(z) before exponentiation. The Flash Attent"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-spacetime-patch","p":"Glossary","t":"Spacetime Patch","b":"A patch spanning both spatial extent and several adjacent video frames, used as the token unit for video generation. Lets one model handle variable durations, resolutions and aspect ratios, since the sequence length simply varies with the clip."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-sparse-autoencoder-sae","p":"Glossary","t":"Sparse Autoencoder (SAE)","b":"A wide, sparsely activating layer trained to reconstruct a network's internal activations, forcing each learned feature onto a single interpretable direction. The principal tool against superposition, and the source of large dictionaries of named, steerable features in production"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-speculative-decoding","p":"Glossary","t":"Speculative Decoding","b":"An inference speedup technique where a small draft model generates K candidate tokens, then the large target model verifies all K in one parallel forward pass. Accepted tokens are output; on rejection, the target model samples a corrected token. Effective speedup: ~2–4× when the "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-superposition","p":"Glossary","t":"Superposition","b":"The encoding of far more concepts than a layer has dimensions, as overlapping nearly-orthogonal directions in activation space. Efficient for the network and disastrous for interpretation: any individual neuron participates in many unrelated concepts, so reading neurons directly "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-swiglu","p":"Glossary","t":"SwiGLU","b":"The FFN variant used in LLaMA, PaLM, and most modern LLMs: SwiGLU(x) = W₂(SiLU(W₁x) ⊙ W₃x). Uses three weight matrices (vs two for standard FFN) with a smaller hidden dimension, keeping parameter count equal while improving quality. The gating mechanism (W₃x) allows the network t"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-sycophancy","p":"Glossary","t":"Sycophancy","b":"A model agreeing with the user against the evidence, or abandoning a correct answer under mild pushback. A direct consequence of optimising for human approval, since people approve of agreement. A measurement failure before it is a behaviour failure, and the clearest everyday exa"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-task-horizon","p":"Glossary","t":"Task Horizon","b":"The length of task — measured by how long a skilled human would take — that a model can complete unaided at a given success rate. A more informative measure of agent progress than benchmark accuracy, and one that has been improving rapidly."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-teleoperation","p":"Glossary","t":"Teleoperation","b":"A human directly driving a robot through a task, using a VR rig or puppet controller, to record demonstration data. The highest-quality source of robot training data and the least scalable, being bounded by human hours and physical hardware."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-temperature-sampling","p":"Glossary","t":"Temperature (Sampling)","b":"A hyperparameter that scales the logits before softmax: P(v) ∝ exp(z_v/T). T<1: sharper distribution (more deterministic, less creative). T=1: model's trained distribution. T>1: flatter distribution (more random). T→0: greedy decoding (argmax). Common production values: T=0.7–1.0"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tensor-core","p":"Glossary","t":"Tensor Core","b":"Specialised execution unit in NVIDIA Volta+ GPUs (2017–present) that computes D = A×B+C on small matrices (e.g. 16×8×16) in a single instruction. H100 4th-gen Tensor Cores support BF16, FP16, FP8, INT8, INT4 inputs. Peak BF16 throughput: 1,979 TFLOPS. 6–16× faster than CUDA cores"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tensor-parallelism","p":"Glossary","t":"Tensor Parallelism","b":"A distributed training/inference strategy that shards individual weight matrices across multiple GPUs. Each GPU holds a fraction of each matrix; they synchronise via all-reduce within each transformer layer. Requires high-bandwidth interconnect (NVLink) — inter-server InfiniBand "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tokenisation","p":"Glossary","t":"Tokenisation","b":"Converting raw text into a sequence of integer IDs (tokens) from a fixed vocabulary. Modern LLMs use subword tokenisation (BPE, SentencePiece): common words are single tokens, rare words decompose into subwords. Vocabulary size V: 32,000–130,000 tokens. Average English: ~1.3 toke"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tool-use-function-calling","p":"Glossary","t":"Tool Use / Function Calling","b":"Presenting a model with typed schemas describing available functions, so it can emit a structured request that a harness validates, permissions and executes, returning the result into the context. The mechanism underlying every agent."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-top-p-sampling-nucleus-sampling","p":"Glossary","t":"Top-p Sampling (Nucleus Sampling)","b":"Restricts token sampling to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds p (e.g. 0.9). More adaptive than top-k: when the model is confident (peaked distribution), fewer tokens are included; when uncertain (flat distribution), more tokens are included. Standard"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tpu-tensor-processing-unit","p":"Glossary","t":"TPU (Tensor Processing Unit)","b":"Google's custom AI ASIC, deployed at Google scale since 2016. Uses a systolic array architecture for highly efficient GEMM. Programmed via XLA (JAX/TensorFlow). TPU v5p: 459 TFLOPS BF16, 95 GB HBM, 4.8 TB/s inter-chip interconnect (ICI). Pods of 8,960 TPUs are used for Google's l"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-transformer","p":"Glossary","t":"Transformer","b":"The neural network architecture introduced in \"Attention Is All You Need\" (Vaswani et al., 2017) that replaced RNNs as the dominant architecture for language models. Key components: multi-head self-attention, position-wise feed-forward networks, residual connections, layer normal"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-tsmc","p":"Glossary","t":"TSMC","b":"Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company. The world's largest and most advanced pure-play foundry, founded 1987 by Morris Chang. Manufactures chips for NVIDIA, AMD, Apple, Qualcomm, Google, and most other major semiconductor companies. Commands ~60% of global foundry revenue an"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-vanishing-gradient","p":"Glossary","t":"Vanishing Gradient","b":"The problem where gradients become exponentially small as they propagate backward through deep networks. Occurs when activation functions (sigmoid, tanh) have near-zero derivatives at saturation, and these are multiplied together through many layers. Solved by ReLU activations, r"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-vla-vision-language-action-model","p":"Glossary","t":"VLA (Vision-Language-Action Model)","b":"A robot policy built by fine-tuning a pretrained vision-language model to output actions instead of text, conditioned on camera images, proprioception and a natural-language instruction. Web-scale semantic knowledge transfers; motor skill must still be learned from robot data."},{"u":"glossary.html#term-vllm","p":"Glossary","t":"vLLM","b":"An open-source LLM serving engine (UC Berkeley, 2023) that introduced PagedAttention, continuous batching, and prefix caching. Achieves 2–24× higher throughput than naive Hugging Face inference. Supports most open LLMs (LLaMA, Mistral, Qwen, etc.) and multiple hardware backends. "},{"u":"glossary.html#term-warp","p":"Glossary","t":"Warp","b":"The fundamental execution unit in NVIDIA's SIMT model: 32 threads executing the same instruction simultaneously on different data. All 32 threads in a warp advance in lockstep — if they diverge (different branches), they serialize. A GPU SM schedules between warps zero-cost, hidi"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-weight-decay-l2-regularisation","p":"Glossary","t":"Weight Decay (L2 Regularisation)","b":"Adds a penalty term λ/2 × Σwᵢ² to the loss, causing weights to decay toward zero unless the gradient pushes back. Effective update: w ← w(1−ηλ) − η·∇L. Penalises large weights, biasing the model toward simpler solutions. Typical λ: 0.01–0.1 for large LLMs. Implemented in AdamW (A"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-world-model","p":"Glossary","t":"World Model","b":"A learned model that predicts the next state of an environment given the current state and a proposed action, enabling planning by imagination rather than by trial. Video generation models are surprisingly capable world models, but produce sequences that look physical without gua"},{"u":"glossary.html#term-zero-zero-redundancy-optimizer","p":"Glossary","t":"ZeRO (Zero Redundancy Optimizer)","b":"Microsoft DeepSpeed's technique to eliminate redundant copies in data-parallel training. Stage 1: shard optimizer state across ranks (4× memory reduction). Stage 2: + shard gradients (8×). Stage 3: + shard model parameters (P× where P = number of GPUs). With ZeRO-3 on 1,024 GPUs,"},{"u":"hardware.html","p":"Hardware","t":"AI Hardware","b":"AI Hardware — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"hardware.html#why-cpus-cannot-train-ai-models","p":"Hardware","t":"Why CPUs Cannot Train AI Models","b":"In plain terms A CPU is a small team of brilliant generalists who can each solve any problem, one careful step at a time. A GPU is an army of tens of thousands of specialists who can only do arithmetic, but all at once. Training a neural ne"},{"u":"hardware.html#gpu-architecture","p":"Hardware","t":"GPU Architecture","b":""},{"u":"hardware.html#simt-execution-model","p":"Hardware","t":"SIMT Execution Model","b":"A GPU executes threads in groups of 32 called warps . All 32 threads in a warp execute the same instruction simultaneously on different data — Single Instruction, Multiple Thread (SIMT). This is highly efficient when all threads follow the "},{"u":"hardware.html#tensor-cores","p":"Hardware","t":"Tensor Cores","b":"Tensor Cores are specialised matrix-multiply-accumulate (MMA) units introduced in NVIDIA Volta (2017). They compute D = A × B + C on 16×16 or 8×16 matrices in a single operation, far faster than using CUDA cores for the same computation: H1"},{"u":"hardware.html#nvidia-gpu-generations","p":"Hardware","t":"NVIDIA GPU Generations","b":"GPU Year Architecture CUDA Cores BF16 TFLOPS HBM Memory BW NVLink BW TDP A100 SXM4 2020 Ampere 6,912 312 HBM2e 80GB 2,000 GB/s 600 GB/s 400W H100 SXM5 2022 Hopper 16,896 1,979 HBM3 80GB 3,350 GB/s 900 GB/s 700W H200 SXM 2024 Hopper 16,896 1"},{"u":"hardware.html#blackwell-architecture-b200","p":"Hardware","t":"Blackwell Architecture (B200)","b":"The B200 (2025) introduces a dual-die design — two Blackwell GPU dies connected via NVLink with 1.8 TB/s die-to-die bandwidth. Together they function as a single logical GPU: B200 SXM: - 2× Blackwell dies per package (connected via 10 TBs N"},{"u":"hardware.html#the-rack-became-the-unit-of-compute","p":"Hardware","t":"The Rack Became the Unit of Compute","b":"The most consequential change in the Blackwell generation is not the die — it is that NVIDIA stopped selling servers and started selling racks. A GB200 NVL72 rack presents 72 GPUs joined by a single NVLink domain, so that from software's po"},{"u":"hardware.html#the-published-roadmap","p":"Hardware","t":"The Published Roadmap","b":"2020–2022 Ampere → Hopper A100 to H100. Tensor core generations three and four; FP8 arrives with Hopper; the transformer era's workhorses. 2024–2025 Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra Dual-die packages, FP4 tensor cores, HBM3e, and the shift to "},{"u":"hardware.html#google-tpus-custom-ai-asics","p":"Hardware","t":"Google TPUs: Custom AI ASICs","b":"Google developed Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) internally starting in 2015, motivated by the explosive growth of TensorFlow inference compute. Unlike GPUs, TPUs are purpose-built for tensor operations with no pretence of general-purpose co"},{"u":"hardware.html#tpu-architecture-vs-gpu","p":"Hardware","t":"TPU Architecture vs GPU","b":""},{"u":"hardware.html#tpu-systolic-array","p":"Hardware","t":"TPU Systolic Array","b":"A systolic array computes C = A × B by streaming A and B through an array of PEs: Example: 128×128 systolic array Row i of A flows rightward through row i of PEs. Column j of B flows downward through column j of PEs. PE(i,j) accumulates: C("},{"u":"hardware.html#memory-hierarchy","p":"Hardware","t":"Memory Hierarchy","b":"Every GPU operation navigates a memory hierarchy. Bandwidth and capacity trade off at every level: GPU memory hierarchy for the H100. Bandwidth drops by ~1,000× from registers to InfiniBand. A kernel that constantly spills to HBM instead of"},{"u":"hardware.html#high-bandwidth-memory-hbm","p":"Hardware","t":"High Bandwidth Memory (HBM)","b":"Conventional GDDR memory (used in gaming GPUs) achieves 500–700 GB/s by using many narrow channels at high frequency. HBM takes a different approach: stack multiple DRAM die vertically and connect them through-silicon vias (TSVs): HBM3 stru"},{"u":"hardware.html#the-cuda-software-stack","p":"Hardware","t":"The CUDA Software Stack","b":"CUDA (Compute Unified Device Architecture), introduced by NVIDIA in 2006, is the programming model and runtime that makes GPUs programmable for general compute. It is the primary reason NVIDIA dominates AI — AMD's ROCm and Intel's oneAPI ar"},{"u":"hardware.html#cuda-programming-model","p":"Hardware","t":"CUDA Programming Model","b":"CUDA thread hierarchy: Thread: single execution unit, has private registers Warp: 32 threads, executed in lockstep (SIMT) Block: up to 1,024 threads, share L1/SRAM, synchronise cheaply Grid: many blocks, distributed across all SMs automatic"},{"u":"hardware.html#occupancy-and-efficiency","p":"Hardware","t":"Occupancy and Efficiency","b":"Occupancy = active warps / maximum resident warps Higher occupancy hides latency: while one warp waits for memory, the SM switches to another warp instantly (zero context-switch cost). Occupancy limited by: - Register usage: 256K registers/"},{"u":"hardware.html#key-libraries-in-the-cuda-ecosystem","p":"Hardware","t":"Key Libraries in the CUDA Ecosystem","b":"Library Function Why it matters cuBLAS GPU-accelerated BLAS (Basic Linear Algebra Subprograms) Optimised GEMM — the core of DNN compute. NVIDIA hand-tunes for each GPU generation. cuDNN Deep Neural Network primitives (conv, attention, pooli"},{"u":"hardware.html#the-competition-and-why-the-moat-held-so-long","p":"Hardware","t":"The Competition, and Why the Moat Held So Long","b":"In plain terms NVIDIA's real advantage was never only the chips. It was that twenty years of software — libraries, compilers, tuned kernels, tutorials, and the accumulated habits of every machine-learning engineer alive — assumed NVIDIA har"},{"u":"hardware.html#interconnects-summary","p":"Hardware","t":"Interconnects Summary","b":"Interconnect Bandwidth Scope Latency Protocol PCIe Gen5 x16 64 GB/s bidirectional CPU ↔ GPU ~1 μs Standard NVLink 4.0 900 GB/s GPU ↔ GPU (same server) <1 μs Proprietary NVLink-C2C 900 GB/s (B200) CPU ↔ GPU (Grace-Hopper) <1 μs Proprietary I"},{"u":"hardware.html#key-takeaways","p":"Hardware","t":"Key takeaways","b":"GPUs win because neural network training is almost entirely dense matrix multiplication, which is embarrassingly parallel and needs no branch prediction or out-of-order execution. Tensor cores, not CUDA cores, do the real work: they perform"},{"u":"implementation.html","p":"Implementation","t":"AI Implementation with PyTorch","b":"Practical AI implementation with PyTorch: tensors, autograd, training loops, LoRA fine-tuning, mixed precision, and production deployment."},{"u":"implementation.html#why-pytorch","p":"Implementation","t":"Why PyTorch","b":"In plain terms PyTorch is the language the field actually speaks. It gives you multi-dimensional arrays that live on a GPU, and — the crucial part — it records every operation you perform on them so it can automatically work out the gradien"},{"u":"implementation.html#tensors-the-core-data-structure","p":"Implementation","t":"Tensors: The Core Data Structure","b":"A tensor is an n-dimensional array, generalising scalars (rank 0), vectors (rank 1), matrices (rank 2), and higher-dimensional structures. PyTorch tensors are similar to NumPy arrays but can live on GPU memory and participate in automatic d"},{"u":"implementation.html#dtypes-and-memory-cost","p":"Implementation","t":"dtypes and Memory Cost","b":"dtype Bits Range / Precision Typical Use float32 (FP32) 32 ±3.4×10³⁸, ~7 decimal digits Reference; optimizer state bfloat16 (BF16) 16 Same range as FP32, 3 decimal digits Training on Ampere/Hopper GPUs float16 (FP16) 16 ±6.5×10⁴, 3 decimal "},{"u":"implementation.html#autograd-automatic-differentiation","p":"Implementation","t":"Autograd: Automatic Differentiation","b":"PyTorch's autograd engine implements reverse-mode automatic differentiation . Every tensor operation records itself into a computation graph (a directed acyclic graph of Function objects). Calling .backward() on a scalar loss traverses the "},{"u":"implementation.html#no-grad-contexts","p":"Implementation","t":"No-Grad Contexts","b":"During inference, gradient tracking wastes memory and compute. Wrap inference code in torch.no_grad() to disable the autograd engine: model.eval() with torch.no_grad(): logits = model(input_ids) probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=-1) For fin"},{"u":"implementation.html#building-models-with-nn-module","p":"Implementation","t":"Building Models with nn.Module","b":"All neural networks in PyTorch inherit from torch.nn.Module . A Module encapsulates parameters, child modules, and a forward() method that defines the computation. Modules compose: a Module can contain other Modules, and PyTorch automatical"},{"u":"implementation.html#key-nn-primitives","p":"Implementation","t":"Key nn Primitives","b":"Module Operation Parameters nn.Linear(in, out) y = xWᵀ + b in×out weights + out biases nn.Embedding(V, d) Token ID → dense vector V×d lookup table nn.LayerNorm(d) Normalise over feature dim 2d (scale γ + shift β) nn.MultiheadAttention(d, h)"},{"u":"implementation.html#the-training-loop","p":"Implementation","t":"The Training Loop","b":"A complete PyTorch training loop has five components: the dataset (raw data), the DataLoader (batching/shuffling), the model , the loss function , and the optimizer . Each training step follows the same four-step pattern: Forward pass — run"},{"u":"implementation.html#gradient-clipping","p":"Implementation","t":"Gradient Clipping","b":"clip_grad_norm_ caps the global gradient norm to max_norm before the optimizer step, preventing the exploding gradient problem that can destabilise training on long sequences. The norm is computed across all parameters: ‖g‖₂ = √(Σᵢ gᵢ²) If "},{"u":"implementation.html#learning-rate-schedulers","p":"Implementation","t":"Learning Rate Schedulers","b":"from torch.optim.lr_scheduler import CosineAnnealingLR, LinearLR, SequentialLR # Linear warmup for 100 steps then cosine decay to 5% of base LR warmup = LinearLR(optimizer, start_factor=0.01, total_iters=100) cosine = CosineAnnealingLR(opti"},{"u":"implementation.html#mixed-precision-training","p":"Implementation","t":"Mixed Precision Training","b":"Training in BF16 halves memory use and doubles throughput on Ampere/Hopper GPUs versus FP32, because tensor cores run at 2× the FLOP rate for 16-bit operations. PyTorch's torch.amp module handles this automatically — the forward and backwar"},{"u":"implementation.html#fine-tuning-pretrained-models","p":"Implementation","t":"Fine-Tuning Pretrained Models","b":"Training a large model from scratch requires millions of GPU-hours. For most applications, the practical path is to fine-tune a pretrained model on task-specific data. The Hugging Face transformers library provides pretrained models and tok"},{"u":"implementation.html#full-fine-tuning","p":"Implementation","t":"Full Fine-Tuning","b":"from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer model_id = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\" tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id) model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained( model_id, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16, device"},{"u":"implementation.html#lora-low-rank-adaptation","p":"Implementation","t":"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation","b":"Fine-tuning all weights of a 7B model requires ~14 GB for weights plus ~56 GB for FP32 optimizer state — impractical on most hardware. LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) freezes the original weights and injects low-rank trainable update matrices into a"},{"u":"implementation.html#when-to-use-each-approach","p":"Implementation","t":"When to Use Each Approach","b":"Method GPU RAM (7B model) Trainable Params Best For Full fine-tuning ~80–160 GB 100% Max accuracy, sufficient hardware LoRA (BF16) ~16–20 GB 0.1–1% Task adaptation with 1–2 GPUs QLoRA (INT4 + LoRA) ~6–10 GB 0.1–1% Consumer GPU fine-tuning P"},{"u":"implementation.html#datasets-and-dataloaders","p":"Implementation","t":"Datasets and DataLoaders","b":"The torch.utils.data module provides the contract for streaming training data efficiently. A Dataset implements __len__ and __getitem__ . A DataLoader wraps it with batching, shuffling, and multi-process prefetching. from torch.utils.data i"},{"u":"implementation.html#production-deployment","p":"Implementation","t":"Production Deployment","b":"Research code uses Python's dynamism freely. Production code must be fast, portable, and able to run without the full Python runtime. PyTorch offers several compilation paths."},{"u":"implementation.html#torch-compile-pytorch-2-0","p":"Implementation","t":"torch.compile (PyTorch 2.0+)","b":"The simplest path to faster inference. Wrapping a model with torch.compile() triggers Dynamo (a Python bytecode interpreter) to capture the computation graph and hand it to Inductor (the default backend), which generates optimised Triton or"},{"u":"implementation.html#onnx-export","p":"Implementation","t":"ONNX Export","b":"ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) is a portable IR for models. Export once, run anywhere — ONNX Runtime, TensorRT, CoreML, OpenVINO. Essential for deploying to mobile, edge, or non-NVIDIA hardware. dummy = torch.zeros(1, 128, dtype=torch."},{"u":"implementation.html#torchserve","p":"Implementation","t":"TorchServe","b":"TorchServe is PyTorch's model serving framework. It handles REST/gRPC endpoints, batching, multiple model versions, and metrics out of the box. # Package model artifact torch-model-archiver --model-name mymodel --version 1.0 \\ --model-file "},{"u":"implementation.html#fine-tuning-is-usually-the-wrong-first-move","p":"Implementation","t":"Fine-Tuning Is Usually the Wrong First Move","b":"In plain terms Fine-tuning is the most requested and least often necessary technique in applied AI. Before you spend a week on it, work through the cheaper options in order — most teams find their problem was solved three steps before they "},{"u":"implementation.html#the-pytorch-ecosystem","p":"Implementation","t":"The PyTorch Ecosystem","b":"PyTorch's value extends well beyond the core library. A rich ecosystem of libraries handles data, training orchestration, model hubs, and production serving."},{"u":"implementation.html#distributed-training-with-pytorch","p":"Implementation","t":"Distributed Training with PyTorch","b":"Single-GPU training is insufficient for models beyond a few billion parameters. PyTorch provides several distributed strategies through torch.distributed ."},{"u":"implementation.html#data-parallel-ddp","p":"Implementation","t":"Data Parallel (DDP)","b":"DistributedDataParallel replicates the full model on each GPU. Each GPU processes a different micro-batch; gradients are all-reduced (summed and averaged) across GPUs at the end of the backward pass. Scales efficiently to ~8–16 GPUs; commun"},{"u":"implementation.html#model-parallel-and-pipeline-parallel","p":"Implementation","t":"Model Parallel and Pipeline Parallel","b":"When a model doesn't fit on a single GPU, its layers are split across devices. Pipeline parallelism splits the model into stages — GPU 0 runs layers 0–11, GPU 1 runs layers 12–23, etc. — and uses micro-batches to keep all stages active simu"},{"u":"implementation.html#fsdp-fully-sharded-data-parallel","p":"Implementation","t":"FSDP: Fully Sharded Data Parallel","b":"FSDP (PyTorch 1.12+) shards model parameters, gradients, and optimizer state across all GPUs, rather than replicating them. Each GPU holds only 1/N of each tensor. During forward and backward passes, FSDP all-gathers the full parameter shar"},{"u":"implementation.html#building-on-models-you-do-not-host","p":"Implementation","t":"Building on Models You Do Not Host","b":"Most production AI systems never train anything. They call an API, and the engineering is everything around that call. Some of it is unfamiliar to people arriving from conventional backend work: Structure the output. Constrained decoding an"},{"u":"implementation.html#key-takeaways","p":"Implementation","t":"Key takeaways","b":"PyTorch's autograd records operations as you run them, so gradients come free from ordinary Python control flow — the reason it displaced graph-first frameworks. Mixed precision keeps BF16 activations with FP32 master weights: roughly half "},{"u":"index.html","p":"Home","t":"Learn AI","b":"A deep technical guide to modern AI: neural networks, transformers, training and inference, agents, robotics, hardware, data centers, semiconductor foundries, economics, and governance."},{"u":"index.html#foundations","p":"Home","t":"Foundations","b":"How the models work. Start here if you want to understand anything else on this site. 🧠"},{"u":"index.html#neural-networks","p":"Home","t":"Neural Networks","b":"The artificial neuron, weight matrices, activation functions, backpropagation and the chain rule — the mathematical foundation everything else is built on. Start here → 📖"},{"u":"index.html#large-language-models","p":"Home","t":"Large Language Models","b":"The transformer in detail: scaled dot-product attention, multi-head and grouped-query attention, RoPE, mixture-of-experts, long context, and what emerges at scale. Explore → ⚙️"},{"u":"index.html#training-models","p":"Home","t":"Training Models","b":"Optimisers, loss landscapes, scaling laws, mixed precision, distributed training — and the post-training stack of SFT, RLHF, DPO and reinforcement learning on verifiable rewards. Explore → ⚡"},{"u":"index.html#inference","p":"Home","t":"Inference","b":"Autoregressive generation, KV caching, quantisation, speculative decoding, prefill versus decode, reasoning-time compute, and the throughput/latency tradeoff. Explore → 🎨"},{"u":"index.html#beyond-text-new","p":"Home","t":"Beyond Text New","b":"Diffusion and flow matching, patch embeddings, contrastive vision-language training, video generation, neural audio codecs, native multimodal models, and world models. Explore →"},{"u":"index.html#systems","p":"Home","t":"Systems","b":"What happens when models are given tools, bodies, and jobs to do. 🤖"},{"u":"index.html#agentic-ai-new","p":"Home","t":"Agentic AI New","b":"The agent loop, tool design, context engineering, multi-agent systems, error compounding, prompt injection and the lethal trifecta — and how humans direct and supervise agents. Explore → 🦾"},{"u":"index.html#ai-robotics-new","p":"Home","t":"AI & Robotics New","b":"Vision-language-action models, the robot data problem, simulation and the reality gap, humanoid hardware and control hierarchies, and the economics of physical labour. Explore → 🛠️"},{"u":"index.html#implementation","p":"Home","t":"Implementation","b":"PyTorch tensors and autograd, the training loop, LoRA fine-tuning, mixed precision, torch.compile, ONNX export, and distributed training with DDP and FSDP. Explore → 📏"},{"u":"index.html#evaluation-benchmarks-new","p":"Home","t":"Evaluation & Benchmarks New","b":"Benchmark saturation and contamination, preference arenas, LLM-as-judge and its biases, agent evaluation, pass@k versus pass^k, and how to build evals you can trust. Explore →"},{"u":"index.html#infrastructure","p":"Home","t":"Infrastructure","b":"The physical machine. Everything above runs on this, and its limits are the industry's limits. 💾"},{"u":"index.html#ai-hardware","p":"Home","t":"AI Hardware","b":"GPU, TPU and custom-ASIC architecture, tensor cores, the Hopper → Blackwell → Rubin roadmap, HBM bandwidth, NVLink and the CUDA software moat. Explore → 🏭"},{"u":"index.html#training-data-centers","p":"Home","t":"Training Data Centers","b":"Cluster topology, NVLink domains, InfiniBand and Ethernet fabrics, parallel file systems, gigawatt power budgets, liquid cooling, and fault tolerance on month-long runs. Explore → 🌐"},{"u":"index.html#inference-data-centers","p":"Home","t":"Inference Data Centers","b":"Serving at global scale: continuous batching, PagedAttention, prefix caching, prefill/decode disaggregation, geographic routing, and cost modelling per million tokens. Explore → 🔬"},{"u":"index.html#semiconductor-foundries","p":"Home","t":"Semiconductor Foundries","b":"EUV lithography, gate-all-around process nodes, yield economics, CoWoS packaging and HBM integration, TSMC and its rivals, and export controls as industrial policy. Explore →"},{"u":"index.html#consequences","p":"Home","t":"Consequences","b":"Who pays for this, what could go wrong, and who gets to decide. 💰"},{"u":"index.html#the-economics-of-ai-new","p":"Home","t":"The Economics of AI New","b":"Startup financing and sovereign capital, staying private, IPOs, circular vendor financing and its 1990s precedent, open weights versus closed models, and the US, EU and China compared. Explore → 🛡️"},{"u":"index.html#safety-governance-new","p":"Home","t":"Safety & Governance New","b":"Alignment as a specification problem, sycophancy and reward hacking, interpretability and superposition, defence in depth, and the EU, US and Chinese regulatory regimes. Explore → 🧭"},{"u":"index.html#learning-paths-new","p":"Home","t":"Learning Paths New","b":"Four routes through this guide, the dependency graph between topics, the maths you actually need, projects that build intuition, and eight things worth unlearning. Start here → 📚"},{"u":"index.html#glossary","p":"Home","t":"Glossary","b":"Searchable definitions for the technical vocabulary used throughout this guide — from action chunking to zero-redundancy optimisers. Reference →"},{"u":"index.html#the-ai-stack-at-a-glance","p":"Home","t":"The AI Stack at a Glance","b":"Each layer below is a page of this guide. Constraints propagate upward — a packaging bottleneck in a Taiwanese fab eventually shows up as a rate limit on an API — and requirements propagate downward, which is why model architects now think "},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Inference Data Centers","b":"Inference Data Centers — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#training-vs-inference-two-different-worlds","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Training vs Inference: Two Different Worlds","b":"In plain terms A training cluster runs one enormous job for months and nobody is waiting on it. An inference fleet runs millions of tiny jobs at once and every one of them has an impatient person or program at the other end. Same hardware, "},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#the-serving-architecture","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"The Serving Architecture","b":"A production LLM serving system has multiple layers between the user and the GPU: Production LLM serving architecture. Requests flow from CDN → load balancer → scheduler → GPU pods. Tokens stream back. The scheduler manages KV cache and bat"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#tensor-parallelism-for-serving","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Tensor Parallelism for Serving","b":"A 70B-parameter model at INT4 quantisation requires 35 GB just for weights. An H100 has 80 GB HBM — it fits. But at BF16, 70B requires 140 GB — needs 2 GPUs minimum. At FP32, 280 GB — needs 4+ GPUs. More importantly, a single H100 running d"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#continuous-batching-and-pagedattention","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Continuous Batching and PagedAttention","b":""},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#the-problem-with-static-batching","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"The Problem with Static Batching","b":"Naive inference servers process a fixed batch of N requests together, wait for all N to complete, then accept the next batch. This \"static batching\" wastes GPU time: a request that finishes early leaves its GPU slot idle until the longest r"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#continuous-batching-orca-2022","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Continuous Batching (Orca, 2022)","b":"Each decode step, the scheduler inserts new requests into available batch slots as existing ones complete — at token granularity, not request granularity. This keeps all GPU batch slots occupied. GPU utilisation improves from 30–50% (static"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#pagedattention-memory-management","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"PagedAttention Memory Management","b":"KV cache for a single request: sequence_len × n_layers × n_heads × d_head × 2 bytes For LLaMA-3 70B, max context 8K, BF16: = 8192 × 80 × 64 × 128 × 2 ≈ 10.7 GB per request at max length Static allocation: reserve max_length × KV_per_token f"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#prompt-prefix-caching","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Prompt Prefix Caching","b":"Many API users prepend a long system prompt to every request (e.g., \"You are a customer service agent for Company X…\"). Without caching, the prefill phase recomputes the KV vectors for this system prompt on every request — wasteful. Prefix "},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#geographic-distribution","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Geographic Distribution","b":"Inference latency has a physical speed-of-light floor. Light travels ~200 km/ms in fibre (with switching overhead). A user in Tokyo querying a server in Virginia adds ~75 ms one-way round-trip before the first token arrives — violating TTFT"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#traffic-routing","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Traffic Routing","b":"Anycast DNS routes users to the geographically nearest PoP. If a PoP is at capacity, requests overflow to the next-nearest. Health checks detect GPU pod failures within seconds and remove unhealthy pods from the routing pool. Blue/green dep"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#cost-modeling","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Cost Modeling","b":"The economics of inference determine pricing and profitability. Key cost driver: GPU hours per token. In plain terms The cost of serving a model is the cost of renting the accelerators divided by how many tokens you can push through them. E"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#where-the-cost-actually-goes","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Where the Cost Actually Goes","b":"Four levers, in rough order of leverage 1. Model choice 100× spread between a frontier model and a distilled small one. Routing easy requests to a cheap model beats every other optimisation. 2. Prefix caching Up to ~10× on the cached portio"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#dedicated-inference-chips","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Dedicated Inference Chips","b":"Chip Company Tokens/sec (70B) Approach H100 SXM5 NVIDIA ~1,200 (batch 512) General GPU, CUDA ecosystem LPU Groq ~800 (single request) SRAM-only, no HBM, deterministic Inferentia2 AWS ~600 Custom ASIC, tight AWS integration TPU v5e / v7 Goog"},{"u":"inference-datacenter.html#key-takeaways","p":"Inference Data Centers","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Serving optimises latency per request under a throughput constraint; training optimises total throughput. Nearly every design difference follows from that. Continuous batching and PagedAttention together took GPU memory utilisation from rou"},{"u":"inference.html","p":"Inference","t":"Inference","b":"Inference — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"inference.html#what-inference-is","p":"Inference","t":"What Inference Is","b":"In plain terms Training is building the model; inference is using it. When you send a prompt, the model reads your whole message in one efficient parallel pass, then produces the reply one token at a time — and each new token requires readi"},{"u":"inference.html#autoregressive-generation","p":"Inference","t":"Autoregressive Generation","b":"A language model does not generate the full response in one shot. It generates one token at a time, each token conditioned on all previous tokens — including those it just generated: Given prompt x₁, x₂, …, xₙ: Step 1: P(t_{n+1} | x₁…xₙ) → "},{"u":"inference.html#kv-cache","p":"Inference","t":"KV Cache","b":"In the transformer's self-attention mechanism, every token attends to every previous token. Computing attention naively at every decode step would require recomputing the Key (K) and Value (V) vectors for all past tokens from scratch — O(n²"},{"u":"inference.html#pagedattention","p":"Inference","t":"PagedAttention","b":"Standard KV caching allocates a contiguous block of memory per request at maximum context length — leading to severe internal fragmentation when requests have variable lengths. The vLLM framework introduced PagedAttention (2023), which mana"},{"u":"inference.html#sampling-strategies","p":"Inference","t":"Sampling Strategies","b":"The model outputs a distribution over the vocabulary — a vector of logits zᵥ for each of V possible next tokens. The sampler converts this into a single token choice:"},{"u":"inference.html#greedy-decoding","p":"Inference","t":"Greedy Decoding","b":"token = argmax_v softmax(z_v) [always picks the most probable token] Fast and deterministic. Poor for open-ended generation — tends toward repetitive, bland text."},{"u":"inference.html#temperature-sampling","p":"Inference","t":"Temperature Sampling","b":"P(token = v) = softmax(z_v / T)_v = e^{z_v/T} / Σ_w e^{z_w/T} T → 0 : greedy decoding (argmax) T = 1 : sample from model's distribution as trained T > 1 : flatten distribution, more random/creative T → ∞ : uniform distribution over all toke"},{"u":"inference.html#top-k-and-top-p-nucleus-sampling","p":"Inference","t":"Top-k and Top-p (Nucleus) Sampling","b":"Top-k: restrict sampling to the k highest-probability tokens, renormalise, sample. Typical k = 40–100. Top-p: sort tokens by probability (descending). Include tokens until cumulative probability ≥ p, renormalise, sample. Typical p = 0.9–0.9"},{"u":"inference.html#quantisation","p":"Inference","t":"Quantisation","b":"Model weights stored in FP16 or BF16 require 2 bytes per parameter. Quantisation compresses weights to lower precision, reducing both memory footprint and — critically — memory bandwidth consumption during decode (which is the primary bottl"},{"u":"inference.html#activation-weight-vs-weight-only-quantisation","p":"Inference","t":"Activation-Weight vs Weight-Only Quantisation","b":"During decode, the bottleneck is reading weight matrices from HBM into the GPU's SMs — weight-only INT4 (W4A16) maximally reduces this read, then dequantises to FP16 for the actual matrix multiplication. During prefill, compute is the bottl"},{"u":"inference.html#speculative-decoding","p":"Inference","t":"Speculative Decoding","b":"Speculative decoding (Leviathan et al., 2022) uses a small, fast draft model to generate K candidate tokens, which the large target model then verifies in a single batched forward pass: Algorithm: 1. Draft model (e.g. 7B) autoregressively g"},{"u":"inference.html#inference-metrics-and-service-level-objectives","p":"Inference","t":"Inference Metrics and Service-Level Objectives","b":"TTFT Time to First Token — latency from request submission to first token appearing. Includes queueing + prefill. Target: <200 ms for chat. ITL Inter-Token Latency — time between consecutive generated tokens. Target: <30 ms (33 tokens/sec) "},{"u":"inference.html#the-batching-tradeoff","p":"Inference","t":"The Batching Tradeoff","b":"Processing multiple requests simultaneously (batching) increases throughput — the GPU is better utilised. But it adds latency for individual requests (requests wait in queue, longer decodes, larger KV caches). The optimal batch size maximis"},{"u":"inference.html#serving-in-the-reasoning-era","p":"Inference","t":"Serving in the Reasoning Era","b":"In plain terms Models that think before answering changed what serving means. A chatbot reply might be 300 tokens. A reasoning model may generate 10,000 tokens of internal working that the user never sees before writing its actual answer. S"},{"u":"inference.html#prefill-and-decode-want-different-hardware","p":"Inference","t":"Prefill and Decode Want Different Hardware","b":"The two phases of inference have opposite profiles. Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel: it is compute-bound, saturates tensor cores, and wants raw FLOPS. Decode generates one token at a time: it is memory-bandwidth-bound, leaves"},{"u":"inference.html#output-tokens-dominate-the-bill","p":"Inference","t":"Output Tokens Dominate the Bill","b":"Providers price input tokens far below output tokens — often by a factor of three to ten — and the asymmetry is not arbitrary. Input tokens are processed once, in parallel, at high arithmetic intensity. Output tokens each require a full pas"},{"u":"inference.html#caching-became-load-bearing","p":"Inference","t":"Caching Became Load-Bearing","b":"Prefix caching was a useful optimisation for chat. For agents it is structural. An agent re-sends a growing transcript on every step, and the stable front of that transcript — system prompt, tool schemas, project conventions — is byte-ident"},{"u":"inference.html#compute-vs-memory-bandwidth-the-core-tradeoff","p":"Inference","t":"Compute vs Memory Bandwidth: The Core Tradeoff","b":"Whether inference is compute-bound or memory-bound determines which optimisations matter. The key ratio is the arithmetic intensity — FLOPs per byte of memory access: Arithmetic intensity = FLOPs / Bytes accessed For matrix-vector multiplic"},{"u":"inference.html#key-takeaways","p":"Inference","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Inference has two phases with opposite bottlenecks: prefill is compute-bound, decode is memory-bandwidth-bound. Almost everything else follows from this. The KV cache turns quadratic recomputation into linear memory growth, and then becomes"},{"u":"learn.html","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Learning Paths","b":"Where to start in this guide depending on what you want: reading routes for newcomers, engineers, builders and analysts, plus the maths you actually need, projects, and misconceptions to unlearn."},{"u":"learn.html#how-this-guide-is-built","p":"Learning Paths","t":"How This Guide Is Built","b":"Every technical page follows the same shape, so you can calibrate how deeply to read: An \"In plain terms\" box opens each major idea. If you read only these, you will still come away with an accurate mental model. They assume nothing. The ma"},{"u":"learn.html#four-routes-through","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Four Routes Through","b":"🧭"},{"u":"learn.html#the-curious-newcomer","p":"Learning Paths","t":"The Curious Newcomer","b":"You keep reading about AI and want to actually understand it rather than absorb headlines. No maths required beyond what you remember from school. ~3 hours → ⚙️"},{"u":"learn.html#the-engineer","p":"Learning Paths","t":"The Engineer","b":"You write software and want to understand these systems at the level you understand databases or networks — mechanism, cost, and failure modes. ~10 hours → 🛠️"},{"u":"learn.html#the-builder","p":"Learning Paths","t":"The Builder","b":"You are shipping something on top of models and need what actually determines whether it works in production. ~6 hours → 📊"},{"u":"learn.html#the-analyst","p":"Learning Paths","t":"The Analyst","b":"You make decisions about AI — investment, policy, strategy — and need enough technical grounding to tell a real claim from a press release. ~5 hours →"},{"u":"learn.html#route-1-the-curious-newcomer","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Route 1 · The Curious Newcomer","b":"Read the \"In plain terms\" boxes and the main narrative; skip any equation block that looks unfriendly. You will lose no important thread. Neural Networks — what a network is and how learning from mistakes actually works. Everything else dep"},{"u":"learn.html#route-2-the-engineer","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Route 2 · The Engineer","b":"The full guide, bottom-up. Read the equations; they are doing real work. Budget several sittings. Neural Networks → LLMs → Training — the mathematical core. Inference → Implementation — how models run, and how to run them yourself. Hardware"},{"u":"learn.html#route-3-the-builder","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Route 3 · The Builder","b":"You need mechanism where it affects your decisions and can safely skim the rest. Foundries and training data centres are fascinating and will not change your architecture. LLMs — enough architecture to reason about context, cost and capabil"},{"u":"learn.html#route-4-the-analyst","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Route 4 · The Analyst","b":"Enough mechanism to evaluate claims, plus the money and the rules. Neural Networks and LLMs — plain-terms boxes and narrative only. You need the vocabulary and the shape of the thing. Hardware and Foundries — where the physical chokepoints "},{"u":"learn.html#what-depends-on-what","p":"Learning Paths","t":"What Depends on What","b":"The dependency graph. Neural Networks and LLMs are the only genuine prerequisites; everything else is a matter of how much context you want before you start."},{"u":"learn.html#the-maths-you-actually-need","p":"Learning Paths","t":"The Maths You Actually Need","b":"In plain terms Far less than people assume. Almost all of deep learning is multiplying grids of numbers together and nudging them in the direction that reduces error. If you understand what a matrix multiplication does and what a derivative"},{"u":"learn.html#things-to-unlearn","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Things to Unlearn","b":"Some widely repeated statements about AI are wrong, or true in a way that misleads. Correcting these early saves a lot of confusion later. Common claim What is actually true \"It's just autocomplete\" The training objective is next-token pred"},{"u":"learn.html#projects-that-teach","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Projects That Teach","b":"Reading gets you a model of the field; building gets you an intuition for it. These are ordered by effort, and each targets a specific misunderstanding."},{"u":"learn.html#keeping-up-without-drowning","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Keeping Up Without Drowning","b":"The volume of AI output is unmanageable and getting worse. A few habits that hold up: Prioritise mechanism over news. A model release is obsolete in months; understanding why attention is quadratic is permanent. This whole guide is organise"},{"u":"learn.html#key-takeaways","p":"Learning Paths","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Read Neural Networks and LLMs first regardless of route. Everything else is optional and reorderable. Plain-terms boxes carry the ideas; equations carry the precision. Take whichever you need and skip the other without guilt. The maths barr"},{"u":"llms.html","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Large Language Models","b":"Large Language Models — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"llms.html#from-rnns-to-transformers","p":"Large Language Models","t":"From RNNs to Transformers","b":"In plain terms Before 2017, a language model read a sentence the way you do — one word at a time, carrying a summary of everything so far in its head. That summary kept getting blurrier, so anything said fifty words ago was effectively forg"},{"u":"llms.html#scaled-dot-product-attention","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Scaled Dot-Product Attention","b":"The core operation of a transformer. Given a sequence of token representations packed into a matrix X ∈ ℝ^{n×d} (n tokens, d dimensions), three linear projections produce Query, Key, and Value matrices: Q = X W_Q W_Q ∈ ℝ^{d × d_k} K = X W_K"},{"u":"llms.html#causal-masking","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Causal Masking","b":"During training (and in decoder-only models like GPT/LLaMA/Claude), each token may only attend to previous tokens — not future ones. This is implemented by adding −∞ to the upper triangle of QKᵀ before softmax, so e^{−∞} = 0 after softmax: "},{"u":"llms.html#multi-head-attention","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Multi-Head Attention","b":"A single attention head can only model one type of relationship at a time. Multi-head attention runs H independent attention operations in parallel, each with its own projection matrices, then concatenates and projects results: head_h = Att"},{"u":"llms.html#grouped-query-attention-gqa","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Grouped Query Attention (GQA)","b":"Standard MHA replicates K and V matrices for every head — expensive for KV cache. Grouped Query Attention (Ainslie et al., 2023, used in LLaMA-3, Mistral) groups Q heads and shares a single K, V per group: G groups, H query heads, H/G key-v"},{"u":"llms.html#the-transformer-block","p":"Large Language Models","t":"The Transformer Block","b":"Each of the L transformer layers (blocks) applies two sub-layers in sequence, each followed by residual addition and layer normalisation: x' = x + Attention(LN(x)) [multi-head self-attention + residual] x'' = x' + FFN(LN(x')) [feed-forward "},{"u":"llms.html#feed-forward-network-ffn","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Feed-Forward Network (FFN)","b":"The FFN applies two linear projections with a nonlinearity in between. It constitutes ~2/3 of parameters in a transformer: FFN(x) = W₂ · SiLU(W₁x) ⊙ (W₃x) [SwiGLU variant, used in LLaMA] W₁ ∈ ℝ^{4d × d}, W₂ ∈ ℝ^{d × 4d}, W₃ ∈ ℝ^{4d × d} Int"},{"u":"llms.html#positional-encoding","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Positional Encoding","b":"Self-attention is permutation-invariant — the same result regardless of token order. Positional encoding injects order information. Modern LLMs use Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) : RoPE rotates the Query and Key vectors for position m "},{"u":"llms.html#scale-and-the-emergence-of-capability","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Scale and the Emergence of Capability","b":"Model capability scales as a smooth power law with parameters, data, and compute — until it doesn't. Emergent abilities are qualitative capability jumps that appear discontinuously at specific scale thresholds, not predicted by interpolatin"},{"u":"llms.html#notable-model-scale","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Notable Model Scale","b":"175B GPT-3 (2020) — dense, 96 layers, d=12,288. The model that made scale the story 405B Llama 3.1 405B (2024) — the largest widely released dense model 671B / 37B DeepSeek-V3 — total vs active parameters. Sparsity is now the norm ~1T+ Fron"},{"u":"llms.html#mixture-of-experts-moe","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Mixture of Experts (MoE)","b":"MoE replaces each FFN with E independent expert networks. A learned router network selects the top-k experts (typically k=2) for each token: Router: g(x) = TopK(softmax(W_r · x), k) scores each expert for token x MoE-FFN(x) = Σ_{i ∈ TopK} g"},{"u":"llms.html#attention-variants-that-shrink-the-kv-cache","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Attention Variants That Shrink the KV Cache","b":"Grouped-query attention was the first widely adopted response to KV cache pressure. Two further approaches now matter: Multi-head latent attention (MLA). Rather than sharing key-value heads, compress keys and values into a low-rank latent v"},{"u":"llms.html#reasoning-models-and-inference-time-compute","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Reasoning Models and Inference-Time Compute","b":"In plain terms For years, making a model better meant making it bigger and training it on more text. Then a third option appeared: let the model think for longer before answering. A model that writes out several thousand tokens of working —"},{"u":"llms.html#context-length-and-its-challenges","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Context Length and Its Challenges","b":"The attention matrix QKᵀ ∈ ℝ^{n×n} scales quadratically with sequence length n. Memory and compute cost of attention: Attention memory: n² × n_heads × sizeof(float) per layer At n=128,000 tokens, H=64 heads, BF16: = (128,000)² × 64 × 2 byte"},{"u":"llms.html#key-takeaways","p":"Large Language Models","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Attention replaced recurrence by letting every position look at every other position directly — which removes the distance penalty and makes training parallel. Attention is O(n²) in sequence length. Nearly every architectural innovation sin"},{"u":"multimodal.html","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Beyond Text: Diffusion, Vision & World Models","b":"How AI handles images, video and audio: patch embeddings, contrastive vision-language training, diffusion and flow matching, video generation, speech, native multimodal transformers, and world models."},{"u":"multimodal.html#everything-is-tokens","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Everything Is Tokens","b":"In plain terms A transformer does not know what text is. It processes sequences of vectors. If you can turn something — an image, a second of audio, a video frame, a robot's joint angles — into a sequence of vectors, the same architecture w"},{"u":"multimodal.html#connecting-vision-to-language","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Connecting Vision to Language","b":"Turning an image into tokens is not enough; the model must relate those tokens to words. The technique that unlocked this is contrastive learning , best known from CLIP. Contrastive image-text training Take a batch of N (image, caption) pai"},{"u":"multimodal.html#diffusion-generating-by-removing-noise","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Diffusion: Generating by Removing Noise","b":"In plain terms To make an image, start with pure static and repeatedly ask a neural network: \"if this were a slightly noisy picture of a fox in a forest, what would the noise be?\" Subtract the predicted noise, and repeat. After twenty or fi"},{"u":"multimodal.html#the-refinements-that-made-it-practical","p":"Beyond Text","t":"The Refinements That Made It Practical","b":"Technique Problem it solves How Latent diffusion Diffusing over raw pixels is ruinously expensive Compress the image ~8× per side with an autoencoder and diffuse in that latent space. Roughly 64× less computation, with little perceptual los"},{"u":"multimodal.html#video-adding-time","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Video: Adding Time","b":"Video is the hardest generative modality, and the difficulty is not merely that there are more pixels. Three problems compound: Temporal consistency. An object must remain the same object across frames — same identity, same colour, same num"},{"u":"multimodal.html#audio-and-speech","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Audio and Speech","b":"Audio splits into two problems with different solutions."},{"u":"multimodal.html#native-multimodal-models","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Native Multimodal Models","b":"Early multimodal systems bolted a vision encoder onto a finished language model with a small adapter between them. It worked, and the seams showed: visual reasoning was noticeably weaker than textual reasoning, and the model could describe "},{"u":"multimodal.html#world-models","p":"Beyond Text","t":"World Models","b":"In plain terms A world model is a neural network that answers \"what happens next?\" Given the current state and a proposed action, it predicts the resulting state. That lets a system plan by imagining consequences instead of trying them — wh"},{"u":"multimodal.html#key-takeaways","p":"Beyond Text","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Every modality becomes a sequence of vectors — image patches, spacetime patches, audio codec tokens — after which the same transformer machinery applies. Contrastive image-text training builds a shared embedding space, which is what lets te"},{"u":"neural-networks.html","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Neural Networks","b":"Neural Networks — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"neural-networks.html#the-artificial-neuron","p":"Neural Networks","t":"The Artificial Neuron","b":"In plain terms An artificial neuron is a weighted vote. It receives several numbers, multiplies each by a weight saying how much that input matters, adds them up, and passes the total through a simple function that decides how strongly to f"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#activation-functions","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Activation Functions","b":"The choice of activation function profoundly affects training dynamics. The dominant activation in modern networks is ReLU and its variants: ReLU(z) = max(0, z) Leaky ReLU(z) = max(αz, z) where α ≈ 0.01 GELU(z) ≈ z · Φ(z) where Φ is the CDF"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#network-architecture","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Network Architecture","b":"Neurons are arranged into layers . The input layer holds the raw features. One or more hidden layers progressively transform the representation. The output layer produces the prediction. In a fully connected (dense) layer, every neuron in l"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#loss-functions","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Loss Functions","b":"In plain terms A loss function is a single number saying how wrong the network currently is. It is the only definition of \"good\" the system ever receives, which makes choosing it the most consequential decision in the whole design — the net"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#mean-squared-error-mse-regression","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Mean Squared Error (MSE) — regression","b":"L MSE = (1/n) Σᵢ (ŷᵢ − yᵢ)² ∂L/∂ŷᵢ = (2/n)(ŷᵢ − yᵢ) ← gradient fed into backprop"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#cross-entropy-classification","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Cross-Entropy — classification","b":"For a C-class problem with one-hot target vector y and softmax output ŷ : L CE = − Σᶜ yᶜ · log(ŷᶜ) For the correct class c*: L CE = −log(ŷ c* ) ∂L/∂zᶜ = ŷᶜ − yᶜ ← combined softmax + cross-entropy gradient The elegance of pairing softmax wit"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#next-token-prediction-loss-language-models","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Next-Token Prediction Loss (Language Models)","b":"L = − (1/T) Σₜ log P(xₜ | x₁, x₂, …, x_{t-1}; θ) where P(·) is the model's softmax distribution over V vocabulary tokens T is the sequence length, θ are the model parameters"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#backpropagation","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Backpropagation","b":"In plain terms The network makes a prediction and it is wrong by some amount. Backpropagation is the bookkeeping that assigns blame: it works backwards through the network figuring out, for every single weight, whether nudging it up or down"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#vanishing-and-exploding-gradients","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Vanishing and Exploding Gradients","b":"During backprop, the gradient at layer ℓ is multiplied by the Jacobian of every subsequent layer. If those Jacobians have spectral norm < 1 (as sigmoid and tanh do at saturation), the gradient shrinks exponentially with depth — vanishing . "},{"u":"neural-networks.html#regularisation","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Regularisation","b":"Without constraint, a network with enough parameters will memorise training data instead of generalising — overfitting . Key regularisation techniques:"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#l2-weight-decay","p":"Neural Networks","t":"L2 Weight Decay","b":"L_total = L_task + (λ/2) Σᵢ wᵢ² ∂L_total/∂wᵢ = ∂L_task/∂wᵢ + λwᵢ Effective update: w ← w(1 − ηλ) − η · ∂L_task/∂w Weight decay penalises large weights, biasing the model toward simpler solutions. λ is a hyperparameter (typical value: 0.01–0"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#dropout","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Dropout","b":"During training, each neuron's activation is set to zero with probability p (typically 0.1–0.5) and scaled by 1/(1−p) to maintain expected values. This prevents co-adaptation — neurons cannot rely on specific other neurons, forcing redundan"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#batch-normalisation","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Batch Normalisation","b":"BN(x) = γ · (x − μ_B) / √(σ²_B + ε) + β μ_B = (1/m) Σᵢ xᵢ (batch mean) σ²_B = (1/m) Σᵢ (xᵢ−μ_B)² (batch variance) γ, β are learned scale and shift parameters ε ≈ 1e-5 for numerical stability Batch norm re-centres activations to zero mean an"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#parameter-count-and-computational-cost","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Parameter Count and Computational Cost","b":"For a fully connected layer with n inputs and m outputs: Parameters: m × n (weights) + m (biases) ≈ mn FLOPs per forward pass: 2mn (mn multiplications + mn additions) For a model with total parameter count P , training on D tokens: Training"},{"u":"neural-networks.html#key-takeaways","p":"Neural Networks","t":"Key takeaways","b":"A neuron is a weighted sum passed through a non-linearity. Everything else is composition and scale. Non-linear activations are what make depth meaningful; without them any network collapses to a single linear layer. The loss function is th"},{"u":"robotics.html","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"AI & Robotics","b":"Embodied AI: vision-language-action models, the robot data problem, simulation and sim-to-real transfer, humanoid hardware and control, and the economics of physical labour."},{"u":"robotics.html#the-hardest-easy-problem","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"The Hardest Easy Problem","b":"In plain terms AI turned out to be good at the things we think of as difficult — proving theorems, writing code, passing professional exams — and bad at the things a toddler does without thinking, like picking up a soft object without crush"},{"u":"robotics.html#from-programmed-robots-to-learned-ones","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"From Programmed Robots to Learned Ones","b":"Industrial robots have worked reliably for fifty years, and they solve a different problem than the one described above. A welding arm repeats a trajectory to a tenth of a millimetre in a cage where nothing unexpected is permitted to happen"},{"u":"robotics.html#vision-language-action-models","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Vision–Language–Action Models","b":"The architectural idea that reorganised the field is straightforward once stated: take a pretrained vision-language model — one that already understands images and instructions from internet-scale data — and fine-tune it to output robot act"},{"u":"robotics.html#the-data-problem","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"The Data Problem","b":"In plain terms There is no internet of robot experience. Every one of the roughly ten trillion words a language model trains on was written by a human for free, over decades. The equivalent robot dataset does not exist and cannot be scraped"},{"u":"robotics.html#simulation-and-the-reality-gap","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Simulation and the Reality Gap","b":"Simulation is the only lever that scales like compute. GPU-accelerated physics engines run thousands of robot instances in parallel; a locomotion policy that would take decades to learn on real hardware trains in hours. Modern robot simulat"},{"u":"robotics.html#world-models-learning-the-simulator","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"World Models: Learning the Simulator","b":"The emerging alternative is to stop hand-writing physics and instead learn a predictive model of the world from video — a neural network that, given the current observation and a proposed action, predicts what happens next. A robot with suc"},{"u":"robotics.html#control-three-loops-at-three-speeds","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Control: Three Loops at Three Speeds","b":"A common misconception is that a single neural network drives the robot. In practice control is layered, because the timescales involved differ by three orders of magnitude and no single system can serve all of them. The control hierarchy. "},{"u":"robotics.html#humanoid-hardware","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Humanoid Hardware","b":"In plain terms A humanoid robot is roughly forty electric motors, a battery, several cameras, a small computer, and a metal skeleton — trying to do what a body does with muscles that are stronger for their weight, joints that heal, and a po"},{"u":"robotics.html#why-humanoid-at-all","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Why Humanoid at All?","b":"The humanoid form is not obviously optimal, and the debate about it is genuinely unsettled. Both cases are strong."},{"u":"robotics.html#the-economics-of-physical-labour","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"The Economics of Physical Labour","b":"Robot economics are refreshingly concrete compared with the model-layer arguments on the Economics page. A robot either costs less than the labour it replaces or it does not. A payback calculation, with the assumptions made visible Robot ca"},{"u":"robotics.html#safety-in-a-machine-that-can-hurt-you","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Safety in a Machine That Can Hurt You","b":"A language model that fails produces a wrong answer. A 70-kilogram machine that fails can break a person's arm. Robotics inherits an entire discipline of functional safety that the rest of AI has never had to engage with — and it is a disci"},{"u":"robotics.html#where-this-is-going","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Where This Is Going","b":"Achieved Locomotion is largely solved Reinforcement learning in simulation produces robust walking, running, stair-climbing and fall recovery across many robot bodies. What was a research milestone a decade ago now ships on commercial hardw"},{"u":"robotics.html#key-takeaways","p":"AI & Robotics","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Moravec's paradox is the organising fact of robotics: sensorimotor skill is computationally harder than abstract reasoning, and physical mistakes cannot be undone. Vision-language-action models fine-tune internet-pretrained vision-language "},{"u":"safety.html","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Safety, Alignment & Governance","b":"How AI systems are aligned to human intent, what interpretability research can and cannot see, the failure modes that matter, and how the US, EU and China regulate AI."},{"u":"safety.html#what-alignment-actually-means","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"What Alignment Actually Means","b":"In plain terms Training a model is writing down a goal in mathematics and then running an enormous optimisation against it. The model gets extremely good at the thing you wrote down. Alignment is the problem that what you wrote down is neve"},{"u":"safety.html#the-alignment-toolkit","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"The Alignment Toolkit","b":"These techniques are covered mechanically on the Training page; here is what each is actually for , and what it does not solve. Technique What it does What it does not fix Supervised fine-tuning Teaches the format and register of helpful re"},{"u":"safety.html#failure-modes-that-are-already-real","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Failure Modes That Are Already Real","b":"Much public discussion of AI risk concerns speculative futures. The failures below are documented, reproducible, and present in deployed systems today. They are also the empirical foundation for taking the speculative ones seriously. Failur"},{"u":"safety.html#interpretability-looking-inside","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Interpretability: Looking Inside","b":"In plain terms We can watch every number inside a neural network, and that tells us almost nothing, in the same way that watching every neuron fire in a brain would not tell you what someone is thinking. Interpretability is the attempt to f"},{"u":"safety.html#safety-in-deployed-systems","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Safety in Deployed Systems","b":"Model-level alignment is one layer of several. A deployed system wraps it in engineering controls, because no single layer is trusted to hold. Defence in depth. Every layer is porous; the argument for safety rests on their combination, and "},{"u":"safety.html#responsible-scaling-and-capability-thresholds","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Responsible Scaling and Capability Thresholds","b":"The major labs have converged on a common structural idea, published under names such as Responsible Scaling Policies, Preparedness Frameworks, and Frontier Safety Frameworks. The design is worth understanding regardless of what you think o"},{"u":"safety.html#governance-three-regimes","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Governance: Three Regimes","b":"In plain terms Europe wrote a comprehensive law before the technology settled. America mostly did not legislate federally and leaned on competitiveness and executive action. China regulates specific applications quickly and firmly, particul"},{"u":"safety.html#european-union-the-ai-act","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"European Union — the AI Act","b":"The world's first comprehensive AI law, in force since 2024 and phasing in across several years. Its architecture is risk-tiered: Prohibited — social scoring by public authorities, certain biometric categorisation, manipulative techniques e"},{"u":"safety.html#united-states-fragmented-and-competitiveness-led","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"United States — Fragmented and Competitiveness-Led","b":"No comprehensive federal AI statute. Federal policy has shifted decisively toward accelerating deployment and infrastructure and toward maintaining a technological lead, with earlier executive requirements around safety testing substantiall"},{"u":"safety.html#china-fast-targeted-and-content-focused","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"China — Fast, Targeted, and Content-Focused","b":"China regulates specific AI applications quickly and with unusual granularity. Measures cover recommendation algorithms, deep synthesis and generative AI services, with requirements including registration of algorithms and models, security "},{"u":"safety.html#open-weights-and-the-irreversibility-problem","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Open Weights and the Irreversibility Problem","b":"The Economics page treats open weights as a competitive strategy. There is a safety dimension that cuts across it, and it is genuinely difficult."},{"u":"safety.html#what-to-actually-worry-about","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"What to Actually Worry About","b":"Public discussion tends to collapse into either dismissal or catastrophe. A more useful framing separates risks by mechanism and timescale, because they call for entirely different responses. Risk Status Mechanism Misinformation and synthet"},{"u":"safety.html#key-takeaways","p":"Safety & Governance","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Alignment is a specification problem: models optimise what you wrote down, and the gap between that and what you meant widens with capability. Sycophancy, confabulation, reward hacking and unfaithful reasoning are documented today, and are "},{"u":"training-datacenter.html","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Training Data Centers","b":"Training Data Centers — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#scale-of-compute-required","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Scale of Compute Required","b":"In plain terms A training cluster is not a big computer room. It is a single machine the size of a warehouse, in which tens of thousands of accelerators must stay in lockstep — every one waiting for every other, thousands of times per secon"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#intra-node-interconnects-nvlink","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Intra-Node Interconnects: NVLink","b":"Within a single server, multiple GPUs must exchange gradient tensors with extremely high bandwidth. PCIe (the standard CPU-GPU interface) tops out at 64 GB/s bidirectional — far too slow for distributed training. NVIDIA's NVLink is a propri"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#inter-node-interconnects-infiniband","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Inter-Node Interconnects: InfiniBand","b":"Communication between servers uses InfiniBand (IB) , a high-speed low-latency network protocol purpose-built for HPC clusters. Ethernet is used in cloud data centres but has higher latency and lower efficiency for collective communications."},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#rdma-remote-direct-memory-access","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"RDMA: Remote Direct Memory Access","b":"InfiniBand uses RDMA — one GPU can read or write data directly into another GPU's HBM across the network without involving either server's CPU. This eliminates PCIe and CPU bottlenecks: Traditional network: GPU → PCIe → CPU → NIC → network "},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#fat-tree-network-topology","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Fat-Tree Network Topology","b":"A training cluster connects thousands of servers. The topology must provide high aggregate bandwidth and non-blocking communication — any two servers must be able to communicate at near full link speed, regardless of which servers they are."},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#all-reduce-in-practice","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"All-Reduce in Practice","b":"During data-parallel training, all GPUs must sum their gradient tensors and distribute the result. The NCCL library (NVIDIA Collective Communications Library) implements ring-all-reduce on top of NVLink and IB: Ring all-reduce on N GPUs, da"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#storage-parallel-file-systems","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Storage: Parallel File Systems","b":"Training data must be fed to GPUs fast enough to keep them busy. A single H100 processes ~16,000 tokens/second. A 10,000-GPU cluster processes 160 million tokens/second — 320 MB/s of tokenised data. The storage system must sustain far highe"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#power-and-cooling","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Power and Cooling","b":""},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#power-density","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Power Density","b":"H100 SXM5 TDP: 700W DGX H100 (8× H100 + 2× Intel Xeon + networking): ~10.2 kW per server 42U rack with 10 DGX servers: ~102 kW per rack Traditional data centre rack limit: 10–15 kW/rack AI cluster rack limit: 100–200 kW/rack (liquid-cooled)"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#cooling-technologies","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Cooling Technologies","b":""},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#power-sources-and-the-grid-problem","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Power Sources and the Grid Problem","b":"A large AI campus exceeds the capacity of a typical city substation, and increasingly exceeds what a regional grid can absorb on the timescale the operator wants. The responses in use: On-site generation. Gas turbines installed at the site,"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#training-across-multiple-data-centres","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Training Across Multiple Data Centres","b":"When one site cannot supply enough power, the run must span several. This is hard: synchronous data-parallel training assumes an all-reduce across every worker at every step, and a wide-area link has thousands of times the latency and a fra"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#fault-tolerance-on-long-training-runs","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Fault Tolerance on Long Training Runs","b":"A training run on 10,000+ GPUs over 3 months has an extremely high probability of hardware failure. GPU failure rates are ~0.1–1% per month per GPU; on 10,000 GPUs, expect 10–100 failures per month. Training frameworks must: Checkpoint freq"},{"u":"training-datacenter.html#key-takeaways","p":"Training Data Centers","t":"Key takeaways","b":"A training cluster is one tightly coupled machine, not a collection of servers. Synchronisation cost, not raw FLOPS, sets its effective throughput. The interconnect hierarchy — NVLink within a rack domain, InfiniBand or Ethernet between rac"},{"u":"training.html","p":"Training","t":"Training Foundational Models","b":"Training Foundational Models — part of the Learn AI guide."},{"u":"training.html#the-training-objective","p":"Training","t":"The Training Objective","b":"In plain terms Training is guess-and-adjust, repeated an astronomical number of times. Show the model some text with the next word hidden. It guesses. Measure how wrong the guess was. Nudge every one of its billions of internal numbers a ti"},{"u":"training.html#optimisers-from-sgd-to-adam","p":"Training","t":"Optimisers: From SGD to Adam","b":""},{"u":"training.html#stochastic-gradient-descent-sgd","p":"Training","t":"Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)","b":"Rather than computing the gradient over the full dataset (prohibitively expensive), SGD estimates it from a random mini-batch B ⊂ 𝒟 of size m: g = (1/m) Σᵢ∈B ∇_θ L(fθ(xᵢ), yᵢ) θ ← θ − η · g η = learning rate (e.g. 3×10⁻⁴ for large models) S"},{"u":"training.html#adam-adaptive-moment-estimation","p":"Training","t":"Adam: Adaptive Moment Estimation","b":"Adam, introduced by Kingma & Ba (2014), is the dominant optimiser for large model training. It maintains per-parameter first and second moment estimates: m_t = β₁ m_{t-1} + (1 − β₁) g_t [first moment: mean of gradient] v_t = β₂ v_{t-1} + (1"},{"u":"training.html#learning-rate-scheduling","p":"Training","t":"Learning Rate Scheduling","b":"A fixed learning rate is rarely optimal. Modern LLM training uses a two-phase schedule: Phase 1: Linear warm-up over W steps η(t) = η_max · t/W for t ≤ W Phase 2: Cosine decay from step W to total steps T η(t) = η_min + 0.5(η_max − η_min)(1"},{"u":"training.html#data-scale-quality-and-preprocessing","p":"Training","t":"Data: Scale, Quality, and Preprocessing","b":"A foundational model's capability is bounded by the quality and diversity of its training data. GPT-3 was trained on 300B tokens; LLaMA 3 on 15 trillion tokens. The primary corpus for language models is the web — Common Crawl alone is hundr"},{"u":"training.html#data-pipeline","p":"Training","t":"Data Pipeline","b":"Crawl and download — Raw HTML from web crawls, books, code repositories, academic papers, Wikipedia. Raw Common Crawl dumps are ~80 TB/year. Extraction and cleaning — Strip HTML, deduplicate near-identical documents using MinHash LSH, remov"},{"u":"training.html#tokenisation-in-detail","p":"Training","t":"Tokenisation in Detail","b":"BPE starts from a character-level vocabulary and iteratively merges the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols. After 32,000 merges, common words are single tokens, rare words are decomposed into familiar subwords. The model never encounter"},{"u":"training.html#mixed-precision-training","p":"Training","t":"Mixed-Precision Training","b":"Full float32 (FP32) requires 4 bytes per parameter. For a 70B parameter model, parameters alone consume 280 GB. Modern training uses mixed precision to reduce memory and accelerate compute on tensor cores: Format Bits Range Use case FP32 32"},{"u":"training.html#distributed-training","p":"Training","t":"Distributed Training","b":"A single GPU cannot hold a 70B-parameter model in training mode. Modern training distributes across thousands of GPUs using three parallelism strategies, often combined (3D parallelism):"},{"u":"training.html#data-parallelism","p":"Training","t":"Data Parallelism","b":"Each GPU holds a full copy of the model. Different GPUs process different batches. After each backward pass, gradients are averaged across all GPUs with an all-reduce collective operation before updating weights. Scales to arbitrary batch s"},{"u":"training.html#tensor-parallelism-megatron-style","p":"Training","t":"Tensor Parallelism (Megatron-style)","b":"Individual weight matrices are sharded across GPUs. A matrix multiplication W × x is split into column-wise or row-wise shards, computed in parallel, and results combined with all-reduce or all-gather. For a transformer attention layer: Q, "},{"u":"training.html#pipeline-parallelism","p":"Training","t":"Pipeline Parallelism","b":"The model is partitioned into L sequential stages across groups of GPUs. Stage 0 processes layer 0–15, stage 1 processes layers 16–31, etc. Micro-batches pipeline through stages, keeping all GPUs busy. Introduces a pipeline bubble (idle tim"},{"u":"training.html#zero-optimizer-zero-redundancy-optimizer","p":"Training","t":"ZeRO Optimizer (Zero Redundancy Optimizer)","b":"Microsoft's ZeRO partitions optimizer state, gradients, and parameters across data-parallel ranks, eliminating redundancy: ZeRO Stage 1: Partition optimizer state → 4× memory reduction ZeRO Stage 2: + Partition gradients → 8× memory reducti"},{"u":"training.html#scaling-laws","p":"Training","t":"Scaling Laws","b":"The Chinchilla scaling laws (Hoffmann et al., DeepMind, 2022) empirically determined the optimal relationship between model size P, training tokens D, and compute budget C: Optimal: D ≈ 20 × P If C = 6PD FLOPs (6 for fwd+bwd+update) Then: C"},{"u":"training.html#how-the-picture-has-changed-since-chinchilla","p":"Training","t":"How the Picture Has Changed Since Chinchilla","b":"In plain terms Chinchilla answered the question \"given a fixed training budget, what is the best model I can train?\" That was the right question when training dominated costs. It is no longer the only question, because three things happened"},{"u":"training.html#the-training-loop","p":"Training","t":"The Training Loop","b":"The training loop. Each forward pass produces predictions, the loss measures error, backpropagation computes gradients via the chain rule, and Adam updates all parameters. This repeats for millions of steps across thousands of GPUs simultan"},{"u":"training.html#pretraining-fine-tuning-and-rlhf","p":"Training","t":"Pretraining, Fine-Tuning, and RLHF","b":""},{"u":"training.html#pretraining-next-token-prediction","p":"Training","t":"Pretraining: Next-Token Prediction","b":"A foundation model is pretrained on a massive, diverse text corpus with the causal language modelling objective: predict the next token given all preceding tokens. This is self-supervised — no human labels needed. The model implicitly learn"},{"u":"training.html#supervised-fine-tuning-sft","p":"Training","t":"Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)","b":"The pretrained model is then fine-tuned on a curated dataset of (instruction, response) pairs written or curated by humans. SFT teaches the model the format of helpful responses and aligns output style with human expectations. Typically inv"},{"u":"training.html#reinforcement-learning-from-human-feedback-rlhf","p":"Training","t":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)","b":"Collect preference data: For a given prompt, sample K responses from the SFT model. Human raters rank the responses (e.g. preferred / not preferred). Train a reward model: A separate neural network r_φ(prompt, response) → scalar is trained "},{"u":"training.html#reinforcement-learning-from-verifiable-rewards","p":"Training","t":"Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards","b":"In plain terms RLHF asks humans which of two answers they prefer. That works until the questions get harder than the humans. The alternative is to train only on problems where a computer can check the answer — does the code compile and pass"},{"u":"training.html#the-modern-post-training-stack","p":"Training","t":"The Modern Post-Training Stack","b":"Stage Data Signal What it produces Pretraining Web-scale text, code, images Next-token prediction Knowledge, fluency, latent capability Mid-training Curated high-quality and domain data Next-token prediction Domain strength, long-context ab"},{"u":"training.html#key-takeaways","p":"Training","t":"Key takeaways","b":"Training is gradient descent on next-token prediction. Everything a model knows is a side effect of getting good at that one objective. Adam's per-parameter adaptive step sizes are what make training stable at this scale, at the cost of two"}];
